window.IELTS_DATA.items.push(...[{"id":"writing-academic-004","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Cities & Housing","title":"House prices in five cities","prompt":"The diagrams provide some information about the housing prices of five cities around the world from 1990 to 2000. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The two bar charts compare changes in house prices in five cities during two periods: 1990–1995 and 1995–2000.\n\nOverall, San Francisco and Beijing <strong class=\"key-phrase\">recorded increases</strong> <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in both periods</strong>, whereas Rome experienced <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a decline</strong> in each. Hong Kong and Sydney moved from positive changes in the first period to falls in the second.\n\nBetween 1990 and 1995, Hong Kong had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the largest rise</strong>, at 5%. Beijing followed with an increase of 4%, while prices rose by 3% in Sydney and 2% in San Francisco. Rome was the only city to record a fall, with a figure of minus 3.5%.\n\nThe pattern changed considerably in the later period. Beijing recorded the strongest increase, at 5.8%, compared with 4% previously. San Francisco&#x27;s figure also rose, from 2% to 3%. By contrast, Hong Kong registered a 6% decline and Sydney a 2% fall. Rome&#x27;s figure <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remained unchanged</strong> at minus 3.5%.\n\nHong Kong therefore showed the largest difference between the two reported changes, moving from a 5% increase to a 6% decrease, a swing of 11 <strong class=\"key-phrase\">percentage points</strong>. Rome was the only city with negative figures in both periods.","phrases":[{"en":"recorded increases","context":"Overall, San Francisco and Beijing recorded increases in both periods, whereas Rome experienced a decline in each. Hong Kong and Sydney moved from positive changes in the first period to falls in the second."},{"en":"in both periods","context":"Overall, San Francisco and Beijing recorded increases in both periods, whereas Rome experienced a decline in each. Hong Kong and Sydney moved from positive changes in the first period to falls in the second."},{"en":"a decline","context":"Overall, San Francisco and Beijing recorded increases in both periods, whereas Rome experienced a decline in each. Hong Kong and Sydney moved from positive changes in the first period to falls in the second."},{"en":"the largest rise","context":"Between 1990 and 1995, Hong Kong had the largest rise, at 5%. Beijing followed with an increase of 4%, while prices rose by 3% in Sydney and 2% in San Francisco. Rome was the only city to record a fall, with a figure of minus 3.5%."},{"en":"remained unchanged","context":"The pattern changed considerably in the later period. Beijing recorded the strongest increase, at 5.8%, compared with 4% previously. San Francisco's figure also rose, from 2% to 3%. By contrast, Hong Kong registered a 6% decline and Sydney a 2% fall. Rome's figure remained unchanged at minus 3.5%."},{"en":"percentage points","context":"Hong Kong therefore showed the largest difference between the two reported changes, moving from a 5% increase to a 6% decrease, a swing of 11 percentage points. Rome was the only city with negative figures in both periods."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-004-1"]},"_displayNo":"#001","summary":"Diagrams provide some information about the housing prices of five cities…"},{"id":"writing-academic-006","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Cities & Housing","title":"Changes in house prices","prompt":"The chart below shows information about changes in average house prices in five different cities between 1990 and 2002 compared with the average house prices in 1989.","answer":"The bar chart compares average house prices in five cities during 1990–1995 and 1996–2002 with their respective 1989 levels.\n\nOverall, three cities recorded negative changes in the first period, whereas only Tokyo remained below its 1989 level in the second. London showed the largest difference between the two periods.\n\nDuring 1990–1995, average prices in Tokyo and London were approximately 7% to 8% below their 1989 levels. New York also recorded a fall, of 5%. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">By contrast</strong>, Madrid and Frankfurt had small positive figures, at around 1% to 2%.\n\nThe figures for 1996–2002 were generally higher. London&#x27;s average was about 12% above its 1989 level, while New York stood 5% above its baseline. Madrid&#x27;s positive difference increased to roughly 4%. Tokyo remained cheaper than in 1989, although its shortfall narrowed to about 5%.\n\nFrankfurt was the exception to the general improvement between the two reported periods. Its positive change became slightly smaller, at around 1% to 2%. These figures compare each period with 1989; they do not represent additional increases on top of the first period&#x27;s prices.","phrases":[{"en":"By contrast","context":"During 1990–1995, average prices in Tokyo and London were approximately 7% to 8% below their 1989 levels. New York also recorded a fall, of 5%. By contrast, Madrid and Frankfurt had small positive figures, at around 1% to 2%."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-006-1"]},"_displayNo":"#002","summary":"Changes in average house prices in five cities from 1990 to 2002 compared with 1989."},{"id":"writing-academic-025","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Crime & Safety","title":"US violent crime","prompt":"The line chart below shows serious violent crime levels from 1978 to 2000 in the US. Serious violent crimes include rape, robbery, aggravated assault and homicide. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The line chart compares four measures of serious violent crime in the United States between 1978 and 2000: the total level, victimisations reported to the police, crimes recorded by the police and arrests. The vertical axis expresses the figures in thousands of offences.\n\nOverall, the total crime level remained the highest series throughout. It rose to a peak in the late 1980s before declining, whereas police-recorded offences and arrests generally increased until the 1990s and then fell towards the end.\n\nThe total stood at approximately 3.5 thousand offences in 1978. After an initial fall to just over 3 thousand in 1980, it climbed to around 4.5 thousand in 1988. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">A downward trend</strong> followed, bringing the figure to about 3 thousand in 2000. Reported victimisations fluctuated at a lower level, generally around 2 to 2.5 thousand in the earlier years, before falling to roughly 1.4 thousand by the end.\n\nPolice-recorded crime increased from about 0.8 thousand in 1978 to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a peak of</strong> approximately 1.8 thousand in 1994, then declined to around 1.5 thousand. Arrests were consistently the lowest measure, rising from roughly 0.3 thousand to about 0.9 thousand in 1996 before ending near 0.7 thousand.","phrases":[{"en":"A downward trend","context":"The total stood at approximately 3.5 thousand offences in 1978. After an initial fall to just over 3 thousand in 1980, it climbed to around 4.5 thousand in 1988. A downward trend followed, bringing the figure to about 3 thousand in 2000. Reported victimisations fluctuated at a lower level, generally around 2 to 2.5 thousand in the earlier years, before falling to roughly 1.4 thousand by the end."},{"en":"a peak of","context":"Police-recorded crime increased from about 0.8 thousand in 1978 to a peak of approximately 1.8 thousand in 1994, then declined to around 1.5 thousand. Arrests were consistently the lowest measure, rising from roughly 0.3 thousand to about 0.9 thousand in 1996 before ending near 0.7 thousand."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-025-1"]},"_displayNo":"#003","summary":"Line chart below shows serious violent crime levels from 1978 to 2000 in…"},{"id":"writing-academic-061","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Crime & Safety","title":"UK vehicle offences","prompt":"The table below shows motor vehicle offences in England and Wales between 1994 and 2004. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The table shows the numbers of six different types of motor vehicle offence recorded in England and Wales between 1994 and 2004.\n\nOverall, speed limit offences and licence offences were the two main types of motor vehicle offence over this period, and speed limit offences <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stood out because they rose so sharply</strong>.\n\nIn 1994, licence, insurance and record keeping offences were the most common type of offence, and the figure rose to 957 thousand in 2004 after a ten-year period of fluctuations. Speed limit offences initially ranked second in 1994 but became the main type of offence by 2004, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">recording a ten-year high of</strong> 2,076 thousand, more than three times the figure ten years earlier. Neglect of traffic signs and directions came third, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">averaging at around</strong> 250 thousand per year.\n\nDangerous, careless or drunken driving was the fourth main type of offence, falling for much of the period from 1994 to 2004 but finally hitting a high for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the period shown</strong> of 231 thousand. Two other types, accident offences and the unauthorised taking or theft of motor vehicles, were the least common, also declining gradually.","phrases":[{"en":"stood out because they rose so sharply","context":"Overall, speed limit offences and licence offences were the two main types of motor vehicle offence over this period, and speed limit offences stood out because they rose so sharply."},{"en":"recording a ten-year high of","context":"In 1994, licence, insurance and record keeping offences were the most common type of offence, and the figure rose to 957 thousand in 2004 after a ten-year period of fluctuations. Speed limit offences initially ranked second in 1994 but became the main type of offence by 2004, recording a ten-year high of 2,076 thousand, more than three times the figure ten years earlier. Neglect of traffic signs and directions came third, averaging at around 250 thousand per year."},{"en":"averaging at around","context":"In 1994, licence, insurance and record keeping offences were the most common type of offence, and the figure rose to 957 thousand in 2004 after a ten-year period of fluctuations. Speed limit offences initially ranked second in 1994 but became the main type of offence by 2004, recording a ten-year high of 2,076 thousand, more than three times the figure ten years earlier. Neglect of traffic signs and directions came third, averaging at around 250 thousand per year."},{"en":"the period shown","context":"Dangerous, careless or drunken driving was the fourth main type of offence, falling for much of the period from 1994 to 2004 but finally hitting a high for the period shown of 231 thousand. Two other types, accident offences and the unauthorised taking or theft of motor vehicles, were the least common, also declining gradually."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-061-1"]},"_displayNo":"#004","summary":"Table below shows motor vehicle offences in England and Wales between 1994…"},{"id":"writing-academic-027","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Education","title":"Education and science participation","prompt":"The charts below show the levels of participation in education and science in developing and industrialised countries in 1980 and 1990.","answer":"The three bar charts show average years of schooling, numbers of scientists and technicians, and research and development spending in developing and developed countries. Figures are given for 1980 and 1990.\n\nIt is clear from the charts that the figures for developed countries are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">much higher than those for</strong> developing nations. Also, the charts show an overall increase in participation in education and science from 1980 to 1990.\n\nPeople in developing nations attended school for an average of around 3 years, with only a slight increase in years of schooling from 1980 to 1990. On the other hand, the figure for industrialised countries rose from nearly 9 years of schooling in 1980 to nearly 11 years in 1990.\n\nFrom 1980 to 1990, the number of scientists and technicians in industrialised countries <strong class=\"key-phrase\">almost doubled to</strong> about 70 per 1000 people. Spending on research and development also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">saw rapid growth</strong> in these countries, reaching $350 billion in 1990. By contrast, the number of science workers in developing countries <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remained below</strong> 20 per 1000 people, and research spending fell from about $50 billion to only $25 billion.","phrases":[{"en":"much higher than those for","context":"It is clear from the charts that the figures for developed countries are much higher than those for developing nations. Also, the charts show an overall increase in participation in education and science from 1980 to 1990."},{"en":"almost doubled to","context":"From 1980 to 1990, the number of scientists and technicians in industrialised countries almost doubled to about 70 per 1000 people. Spending on research and development also saw rapid growth in these countries, reaching $350 billion in 1990. By contrast, the number of science workers in developing countries remained below 20 per 1000 people, and research spending fell from about $50 billion to only $25 billion."},{"en":"saw rapid growth","context":"From 1980 to 1990, the number of scientists and technicians in industrialised countries almost doubled to about 70 per 1000 people. Spending on research and development also saw rapid growth in these countries, reaching $350 billion in 1990. By contrast, the number of science workers in developing countries remained below 20 per 1000 people, and research spending fell from about $50 billion to only $25 billion."},{"en":"remained below","context":"From 1980 to 1990, the number of scientists and technicians in industrialised countries almost doubled to about 70 per 1000 people. Spending on research and development also saw rapid growth in these countries, reaching $350 billion in 1990. By contrast, the number of science workers in developing countries remained below 20 per 1000 people, and research spending fell from about $50 billion to only $25 billion."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-027-1"]},"_displayNo":"#005","summary":"Charts below show the levels of participation in education and science in…"},{"id":"writing-academic-028","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Education","title":"UK school spending","prompt":"The three pie charts below show how the changes in annual spending by a particular UK schools in 1981, 1991, and 2001.","answer":"The three pie charts compare how a school in the UK divided its annual budget between different areas of expenditure in three separate years over a 20-year period.\n\nIt is clear that teachers’ salaries <strong class=\"key-phrase\">made up the largest proportion of</strong> the school&#x27;s spending in all three years (1981, 1991 and 2001). <strong class=\"key-phrase\">By contrast</strong>, insurance was the smallest cost in each year, although its share of the budget grew over the period.\n\nIn 1981, 40% of the school&#x27;s budget went on teachers’ salaries. This figure rose to 50% in 1991, but fell back by 5 <strong class=\"key-phrase\">percentage points</strong> in 2001. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">The proportion of spending on</strong> other workers’ wages <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fell steadily</strong> over the 20-year period, from 28% of the budget in 1981 to only 15% in 2001.\n\nExpenditure on insurance stood at only 2% of the total in 1981, but it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">had reached 8% by 2001</strong>. The percentages for resources and for furniture and equipment, meanwhile, fluctuated. The figure for resources was highest in 1991, at 20%, while the proportion of spending on furniture and equipment <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reached its peak</strong> in 2001, at 23%.","phrases":[{"en":"made up the largest proportion of","context":"It is clear that teachers’ salaries made up the largest proportion of the school's spending in all three years (1981, 1991 and 2001). By contrast, insurance was the smallest cost in each year, although its share of the budget grew over the period."},{"en":"By contrast","context":"It is clear that teachers’ salaries made up the largest proportion of the school's spending in all three years (1981, 1991 and 2001). By contrast, insurance was the smallest cost in each year, although its share of the budget grew over the period."},{"en":"percentage points","context":"In 1981, 40% of the school's budget went on teachers’ salaries. This figure rose to 50% in 1991, but fell back by 5 percentage points in 2001. The proportion of spending on other workers’ wages fell steadily over the 20-year period, from 28% of the budget in 1981 to only 15% in 2001."},{"en":"The proportion of spending on","context":"In 1981, 40% of the school's budget went on teachers’ salaries. This figure rose to 50% in 1991, but fell back by 5 percentage points in 2001. The proportion of spending on other workers’ wages fell steadily over the 20-year period, from 28% of the budget in 1981 to only 15% in 2001."},{"en":"fell steadily","context":"In 1981, 40% of the school's budget went on teachers’ salaries. This figure rose to 50% in 1991, but fell back by 5 percentage points in 2001. The proportion of spending on other workers’ wages fell steadily over the 20-year period, from 28% of the budget in 1981 to only 15% in 2001."},{"en":"had reached 8% by 2001","context":"Expenditure on insurance stood at only 2% of the total in 1981, but it had reached 8% by 2001. The percentages for resources and for furniture and equipment, meanwhile, fluctuated. The figure for resources was highest in 1991, at 20%, while the proportion of spending on furniture and equipment reached its peak in 2001, at 23%."},{"en":"reached its peak","context":"Expenditure on insurance stood at only 2% of the total in 1981, but it had reached 8% by 2001. The percentages for resources and for furniture and equipment, meanwhile, fluctuated. The figure for resources was highest in 1991, at 20%, while the proportion of spending on furniture and equipment reached its peak in 2001, at 23%."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-028-1"]},"_displayNo":"#006","summary":"Three pie charts below show how the changes in annual spending by a…"},{"id":"writing-academic-030","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Education","title":"Children's TV and academic results","prompt":"This tables show data about the amount of television watched by children in different countries (hours per day) and also the average academic achievement of people in these countries (% of people completing each level). Write a report summarising the information. Select and describe the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The tables give information about the number of hours of television watched each day by children in a range of countries, along with the proportion of people in those countries who complete three levels of education.\n\nIt is clear that both television viewing and educational attainment <strong class=\"key-phrase\">vary widely from country to country</strong>. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">The most striking feature is that</strong> the countries whose children watch the most television each day are also those with the highest rates of university completion.\n\nTurning first to television, children in the USA, Canada and the UK watch the most, at 4 hours a day. China and Italy come next with 3 hours, Brazil and India stand at 2 hours each, and Malta and the UAE record only 1 hour per day, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">which is a quarter of the maximum</strong>.\n\nIn terms of academic achievement, 100% of children complete school in Italy, Canada and Malta, and the figures for the other countries are all in the nineties, apart from India at 80%. High school completion <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ranges from</strong> 100% in Malta to figures in the nineties in Italy, Canada and the UAE, and in the eighties in the UK, USA and Brazil. China and India have the lowest rates, at 68% and 60% respectively.\n\nAt university level, the UK has the highest level of completion, at 39%, followed by Canada with 32% and the USA with 29%. Italy, China and Brazil are all in the mid-twenties, while Malta and the UAE stand at 18% and 19%. India <strong class=\"key-phrase\">records the lowest figure of all</strong>, at only 12%.","phrases":[{"en":"vary widely from country to country","context":"It is clear that both television viewing and educational attainment vary widely from country to country. The most striking feature is that the countries whose children watch the most television each day are also those with the highest rates of university completion."},{"en":"The most striking feature is that","context":"It is clear that both television viewing and educational attainment vary widely from country to country. The most striking feature is that the countries whose children watch the most television each day are also those with the highest rates of university completion."},{"en":"which is a quarter of the maximum","context":"Turning first to television, children in the USA, Canada and the UK watch the most, at 4 hours a day. China and Italy come next with 3 hours, Brazil and India stand at 2 hours each, and Malta and the UAE record only 1 hour per day, which is a quarter of the maximum."},{"en":"ranges from","context":"In terms of academic achievement, 100% of children complete school in Italy, Canada and Malta, and the figures for the other countries are all in the nineties, apart from India at 80%. High school completion ranges from 100% in Malta to figures in the nineties in Italy, Canada and the UAE, and in the eighties in the UK, USA and Brazil. China and India have the lowest rates, at 68% and 60% respectively."},{"en":"records the lowest figure of all","context":"At university level, the UK has the highest level of completion, at 39%, followed by Canada with 32% and the USA with 29%. Italy, China and Brazil are all in the mid-twenties, while Malta and the UAE stand at 18% and 19%. India records the lowest figure of all, at only 12%."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-030-1"]},"_displayNo":"#007","summary":"This tables show data about the amount of television watched by children in…"},{"id":"writing-academic-051","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Education","title":"Problems in primary schools","prompt":"The table compares the problems faced by children in two primary schools in 2005 and 2015.","answer":"The table compares two primary schools in terms of the proportions of their pupils who experienced five different educational problems in the years 2005 and 2015.\n\nIt is noticeable that school A had higher proportions of children with all five educational difficulties in both years. However, while school A managed to reduce the incidence of all five problems between 2005 and 2015, school B recorded increases in two categories and unchanged figures in the other three.\n\nIn 2005, 42% of school A&#x27;s pupils found it difficult to follow instructions, whereas only 6% of pupils in school B experienced this problem. Similarly, between 30 and 40 per cent of children attending school A had problems in the areas of spelling, listening, verbal expression and concentration in lessons, while <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the equivalent figures</strong> for school B stood at between 5 and 15 per cent.\n\nIn 2015, the difference between the two schools was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">less pronounced</strong>. Notably, the proportion of children who struggled to follow instructions fell by 24 <strong class=\"key-phrase\">percentage points</strong> in school A, and this school also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">saw falls of</strong> 22, 15, 14 and 5 percentage points in the figures for children who had problems with concentration, listening, verbal expression and spelling. In school B, however, the proportion of children who <strong class=\"key-phrase\">struggled with</strong> spelling and following instructions doubled, to 10% and 12% <strong class=\"key-phrase\">respectively</strong>, and there was no change in the incidence of listening, verbal or concentration problems.","phrases":[{"en":"the equivalent figures","context":"In 2005, 42% of school A's pupils found it difficult to follow instructions, whereas only 6% of pupils in school B experienced this problem. Similarly, between 30 and 40 per cent of children attending school A had problems in the areas of spelling, listening, verbal expression and concentration in lessons, while the equivalent figures for school B stood at between 5 and 15 per cent."},{"en":"less pronounced","context":"In 2015, the difference between the two schools was less pronounced. Notably, the proportion of children who struggled to follow instructions fell by 24 percentage points in school A, and this school also saw falls of 22, 15, 14 and 5 percentage points in the figures for children who had problems with concentration, listening, verbal expression and spelling. In school B, however, the proportion of children who struggled with spelling and following instructions doubled, to 10% and 12% respectively, and there was no change in the incidence of listening, verbal or concentration problems."},{"en":"percentage points","context":"In 2015, the difference between the two schools was less pronounced. Notably, the proportion of children who struggled to follow instructions fell by 24 percentage points in school A, and this school also saw falls of 22, 15, 14 and 5 percentage points in the figures for children who had problems with concentration, listening, verbal expression and spelling. In school B, however, the proportion of children who struggled with spelling and following instructions doubled, to 10% and 12% respectively, and there was no change in the incidence of listening, verbal or concentration problems."},{"en":"saw falls of","context":"In 2015, the difference between the two schools was less pronounced. Notably, the proportion of children who struggled to follow instructions fell by 24 percentage points in school A, and this school also saw falls of 22, 15, 14 and 5 percentage points in the figures for children who had problems with concentration, listening, verbal expression and spelling. In school B, however, the proportion of children who struggled with spelling and following instructions doubled, to 10% and 12% respectively, and there was no change in the incidence of listening, verbal or concentration problems."},{"en":"struggled with","context":"In 2015, the difference between the two schools was less pronounced. Notably, the proportion of children who struggled to follow instructions fell by 24 percentage points in school A, and this school also saw falls of 22, 15, 14 and 5 percentage points in the figures for children who had problems with concentration, listening, verbal expression and spelling. In school B, however, the proportion of children who struggled with spelling and following instructions doubled, to 10% and 12% respectively, and there was no change in the incidence of listening, verbal or concentration problems."},{"en":"respectively","context":"In 2015, the difference between the two schools was less pronounced. Notably, the proportion of children who struggled to follow instructions fell by 24 percentage points in school A, and this school also saw falls of 22, 15, 14 and 5 percentage points in the figures for children who had problems with concentration, listening, verbal expression and spelling. In school B, however, the proportion of children who struggled with spelling and following instructions doubled, to 10% and 12% respectively, and there was no change in the incidence of listening, verbal or concentration problems."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-051-1"]},"_displayNo":"#008","summary":"Table below gives information about the problems faced by children in two…"},{"id":"writing-academic-002","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Environment & Climate","title":"Kolkata climate","prompt":"The climograph below shows average monthly temperatures and rainfall in the city of Kolkata.","answer":"The chart compares average figures for temperature and precipitation over the course of a calendar year in Kolkata.\n\nIt is noticeable that monthly figures for precipitation in Kolkata <strong class=\"key-phrase\">vary considerably</strong>, whereas monthly temperatures <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remain relatively stable</strong>. Rainfall is highest from July to August, while temperatures are highest in April and May.\n\nBetween the months of January and May, average temperatures in Kolkata rise from their lowest point at around 20°C to a peak of just over 30°C. Average rainfall in the city also rises over the same period, from approximately 20mm of rain in January to 100mm in May.\n\nWhile temperatures <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stay roughly the same</strong> for the next four months, the amount of rainfall <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more than doubles</strong> between May and June. Figures for precipitation remain above 250mm from June to September, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">peaking at around 330mm in July</strong>. The final three months of the year <strong class=\"key-phrase\">see a dramatic fall in</strong> precipitation, to a low of about 10mm in December, and a steady drop in temperatures back to the January average.","phrases":[{"en":"vary considerably","context":"It is noticeable that monthly figures for precipitation in Kolkata vary considerably, whereas monthly temperatures remain relatively stable. Rainfall is highest from July to August, while temperatures are highest in April and May."},{"en":"remain relatively stable","context":"It is noticeable that monthly figures for precipitation in Kolkata vary considerably, whereas monthly temperatures remain relatively stable. Rainfall is highest from July to August, while temperatures are highest in April and May."},{"en":"stay roughly the same","context":"While temperatures stay roughly the same for the next four months, the amount of rainfall more than doubles between May and June. Figures for precipitation remain above 250mm from June to September, peaking at around 330mm in July. The final three months of the year see a dramatic fall in precipitation, to a low of about 10mm in December, and a steady drop in temperatures back to the January average."},{"en":"more than doubles","context":"While temperatures stay roughly the same for the next four months, the amount of rainfall more than doubles between May and June. Figures for precipitation remain above 250mm from June to September, peaking at around 330mm in July. The final three months of the year see a dramatic fall in precipitation, to a low of about 10mm in December, and a steady drop in temperatures back to the January average."},{"en":"peaking at around 330mm in July","context":"While temperatures stay roughly the same for the next four months, the amount of rainfall more than doubles between May and June. Figures for precipitation remain above 250mm from June to September, peaking at around 330mm in July. The final three months of the year see a dramatic fall in precipitation, to a low of about 10mm in December, and a steady drop in temperatures back to the January average."},{"en":"see a dramatic fall in","context":"While temperatures stay roughly the same for the next four months, the amount of rainfall more than doubles between May and June. Figures for precipitation remain above 250mm from June to September, peaking at around 330mm in July. The final three months of the year see a dramatic fall in precipitation, to a low of about 10mm in December, and a steady drop in temperatures back to the January average."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-002-1"]},"_displayNo":"#009","summary":"Climograph below shows average monthly temperatures and rainfall in the…"},{"id":"writing-academic-031","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Environment & Climate","title":"European waste management","prompt":"The chart below compares levels of recycling, as well as some less environmentally friendly forms of waste management, in fifteen European countries.","answer":"The bar chart shows the percentage of waste that is recycled, put into landfill or incinerated in various European countries.\n\nLandfill is the largest waste-management category in most of the fifteen countries shown. In fact, while around half of the countries put the majority of their waste into landfill sites, only two countries recycle at least 50% of the waste that they produce.\n\nAustria and Belgium are the highest-recycling of the fifteen countries, recycling around 60% and 50% of their waste <strong class=\"key-phrase\">respectively</strong>. Germany and Sweden also recycle roughly 40% of their waste.\n\nOther European countries recycle far less. Greece, Ireland and the UK, for example, only recycle around 10% of refuse, and put between 80 and 90 per cent of their waste into landfill. Finally,  four countries, namely Luxembourg, Sweden, the Netherlands and Denmark, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">incinerate more waste than they bury</strong>. Denmark has the highest incineration share, at just under 60%, whereas Greece and Ireland show no waste treated in this way.","phrases":[{"en":"respectively","context":"Austria and Belgium are the highest-recycling of the fifteen countries, recycling around 60% and 50% of their waste respectively. Germany and Sweden also recycle roughly 40% of their waste."},{"en":"incinerate more waste than they bury","context":"Other European countries recycle far less. Greece, Ireland and the UK, for example, only recycle around 10% of refuse, and put between 80 and 90 per cent of their waste into landfill. Finally,  four countries, namely Luxembourg, Sweden, the Netherlands and Denmark, incinerate more waste than they bury. Denmark has the highest incineration share, at just under 60%, whereas Greece and Ireland show no waste treated in this way."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-031-1"]},"_displayNo":"#010","summary":"Chart below compares levels of recycling, as well as some less…"},{"id":"writing-academic-035","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Environment & Climate","title":"Waste produced by three companies","prompt":"The graph shows the amounts of waste produced by three companies between 2000 and 2015.","answer":"The line graph compares three companies in terms of their waste output between the years 2000 and 2015.\n\nIt is clear that there were significant changes in the amounts of waste produced by all three companies shown on the graph. While companies A and B <strong class=\"key-phrase\">saw waste output fall over the 15-year period</strong>, the amount of waste produced by company C increased considerably.\n\nIn 2000, company A produced 12 tonnes of waste, while companies B and C produced around 8 tonnes and 4 tonnes of waste material respectively. Over the following 5 years, the waste output of companies B and C rose by around 2 tonnes, but the figure for company A fell by approximately 1 tonne.\n\nFrom 2005 to 2015, company A <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cut waste production by roughly 3 tonnes</strong>, and company B reduced its waste by around 7 tonnes. By contrast, company C saw an increase in waste production of approximately 4 tonnes over the same 10-year period. By 2015, company C&#x27;s waste output <strong class=\"key-phrase\">had risen to 10 tonnes</strong>, while <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the respective amounts of waste</strong> from companies A and B had dropped to 8 tonnes and only 3 tonnes.","phrases":[{"en":"saw waste output fall over the 15-year period","context":"It is clear that there were significant changes in the amounts of waste produced by all three companies shown on the graph. While companies A and B saw waste output fall over the 15-year period, the amount of waste produced by company C increased considerably."},{"en":"cut waste production by roughly 3 tonnes","context":"From 2005 to 2015, company A cut waste production by roughly 3 tonnes, and company B reduced its waste by around 7 tonnes. By contrast, company C saw an increase in waste production of approximately 4 tonnes over the same 10-year period. By 2015, company C's waste output had risen to 10 tonnes, while the respective amounts of waste from companies A and B had dropped to 8 tonnes and only 3 tonnes."},{"en":"had risen to 10 tonnes","context":"From 2005 to 2015, company A cut waste production by roughly 3 tonnes, and company B reduced its waste by around 7 tonnes. By contrast, company C saw an increase in waste production of approximately 4 tonnes over the same 10-year period. By 2015, company C's waste output had risen to 10 tonnes, while the respective amounts of waste from companies A and B had dropped to 8 tonnes and only 3 tonnes."},{"en":"the respective amounts of waste","context":"From 2005 to 2015, company A cut waste production by roughly 3 tonnes, and company B reduced its waste by around 7 tonnes. By contrast, company C saw an increase in waste production of approximately 4 tonnes over the same 10-year period. By 2015, company C's waste output had risen to 10 tonnes, while the respective amounts of waste from companies A and B had dropped to 8 tonnes and only 3 tonnes."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-035-1"]},"_displayNo":"#011","summary":"Graph below shows the amounts of waste produced by three companies over a…"},{"id":"writing-academic-037","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Environment & Climate","title":"Waste production by country","prompt":"The table below shows the amount of waste production (in millions of tonnes) in six different countries over a twenty-year period.","answer":"The table compares the amounts of waste that were produced in six countries in the years 1980, 1990 and 2000.\n\nThe US had the highest reported waste output in all three years. Some <strong class=\"key-phrase\">data are missing</strong> for Ireland and Korea, so a complete comparison of combined totals is not possible in every year. It is also noticeable that Korea was the only country that managed to reduce its waste output by the year 2000.\n\nBetween 1980 and 2000, waste production in the US rose from 131 to 192 million tonnes, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rising trends were also seen in</strong> Japan, Poland and Portugal. Japan&#x27;s waste output increased from 28 to 53 million tonnes, while Poland and Portugal <strong class=\"key-phrase\">saw waste totals increase</strong> from 4 to 6.6 and from 2 to 5 million tonnes <strong class=\"key-phrase\">respectively</strong>.\n\nThe trends for Ireland and Korea were noticeably different from those described above. In Ireland, waste production <strong class=\"key-phrase\">increased more than eightfold</strong>, from only 0.6 million tonnes in 1980 to 5 million tonnes in 2000. Korea, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">by contrast</strong>, cut its waste output by 12 million tonnes between 1990 and 2000. By 2000, Ireland and Portugal had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the lowest figure</strong>s, each generating 5 million tonnes, while the US total was almost forty times as large.","phrases":[{"en":"data are missing","context":"The US had the highest reported waste output in all three years. Some data are missing for Ireland and Korea, so a complete comparison of combined totals is not possible in every year. It is also noticeable that Korea was the only country that managed to reduce its waste output by the year 2000."},{"en":"rising trends were also seen in","context":"Between 1980 and 2000, waste production in the US rose from 131 to 192 million tonnes, and rising trends were also seen in Japan, Poland and Portugal. Japan's waste output increased from 28 to 53 million tonnes, while Poland and Portugal saw waste totals increase from 4 to 6.6 and from 2 to 5 million tonnes respectively."},{"en":"saw waste totals increase","context":"Between 1980 and 2000, waste production in the US rose from 131 to 192 million tonnes, and rising trends were also seen in Japan, Poland and Portugal. Japan's waste output increased from 28 to 53 million tonnes, while Poland and Portugal saw waste totals increase from 4 to 6.6 and from 2 to 5 million tonnes respectively."},{"en":"respectively","context":"Between 1980 and 2000, waste production in the US rose from 131 to 192 million tonnes, and rising trends were also seen in Japan, Poland and Portugal. Japan's waste output increased from 28 to 53 million tonnes, while Poland and Portugal saw waste totals increase from 4 to 6.6 and from 2 to 5 million tonnes respectively."},{"en":"increased more than eightfold","context":"The trends for Ireland and Korea were noticeably different from those described above. In Ireland, waste production increased more than eightfold, from only 0.6 million tonnes in 1980 to 5 million tonnes in 2000. Korea, by contrast, cut its waste output by 12 million tonnes between 1990 and 2000. By 2000, Ireland and Portugal had the lowest figures, each generating 5 million tonnes, while the US total was almost forty times as large."},{"en":"by contrast","context":"The trends for Ireland and Korea were noticeably different from those described above. In Ireland, waste production increased more than eightfold, from only 0.6 million tonnes in 1980 to 5 million tonnes in 2000. Korea, by contrast, cut its waste output by 12 million tonnes between 1990 and 2000. By 2000, Ireland and Portugal had the lowest figures, each generating 5 million tonnes, while the US total was almost forty times as large."},{"en":"the lowest figure","context":"The trends for Ireland and Korea were noticeably different from those described above. In Ireland, waste production increased more than eightfold, from only 0.6 million tonnes in 1980 to 5 million tonnes in 2000. Korea, by contrast, cut its waste output by 12 million tonnes between 1990 and 2000. By 2000, Ireland and Portugal had the lowest figures, each generating 5 million tonnes, while the US total was almost forty times as large."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-037-1"]},"_displayNo":"#012","summary":"Table below shows the amount of waste production (in millions of tonnes) in…"},{"id":"writing-academic-039","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Environment & Climate","title":"Transport carbon emissions","prompt":"The table below shows carbon dioxide emissions from transport in three European countries in 1994 and 2004.","answer":"The table compares the amount of carbon dioxide produced by transport in Germany, France and the United Kingdom in 1994 and 2004, with the totals broken down by type of transport.\n\nOverall, road transport was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">responsible for the majority of emissions</strong> in all three countries, and it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remained the biggest source throughout</strong>. Germany was the only one of the three to cut its total emissions, while both the UK and France failed to reduce carbon dioxide emissions from transport between 1994 and 2004.\n\nGermany, which had higher emissions than either the United Kingdom or France, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">saw its total decrease slightly</strong> from 47.2 to 46.7 million tonnes. By comparison, France recorded an increase of four million tonnes from 34.7 million in 1994, while the United Kingdom saw a smaller rise of 2.5 million tonnes over the same period.\n\nIn the United Kingdom, road transport produced 32.6 million tonnes of emissions in 2004, 2 million more than ten years earlier, while the other three transport sources <strong class=\"key-phrase\">showed no remarkable growth</strong>. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">A similar pattern was seen</strong> in France, where road transport added 3.6 million tonnes to the total emissions within ten years. Germany, by contrast, was the only country of the three to experience a drop in road transport emissions, and its other transport sources also had a lower emission volume, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">with the exception of civil aviation</strong>, where the amount rose to 1.2 million tonnes.","phrases":[{"en":"responsible for the majority of emissions","context":"Overall, road transport was responsible for the majority of emissions in all three countries, and it remained the biggest source throughout. Germany was the only one of the three to cut its total emissions, while both the UK and France failed to reduce carbon dioxide emissions from transport between 1994 and 2004."},{"en":"remained the biggest source throughout","context":"Overall, road transport was responsible for the majority of emissions in all three countries, and it remained the biggest source throughout. Germany was the only one of the three to cut its total emissions, while both the UK and France failed to reduce carbon dioxide emissions from transport between 1994 and 2004."},{"en":"saw its total decrease slightly","context":"Germany, which had higher emissions than either the United Kingdom or France, saw its total decrease slightly from 47.2 to 46.7 million tonnes. By comparison, France recorded an increase of four million tonnes from 34.7 million in 1994, while the United Kingdom saw a smaller rise of 2.5 million tonnes over the same period."},{"en":"showed no remarkable growth","context":"In the United Kingdom, road transport produced 32.6 million tonnes of emissions in 2004, 2 million more than ten years earlier, while the other three transport sources showed no remarkable growth. A similar pattern was seen in France, where road transport added 3.6 million tonnes to the total emissions within ten years. Germany, by contrast, was the only country of the three to experience a drop in road transport emissions, and its other transport sources also had a lower emission volume, with the exception of civil aviation, where the amount rose to 1.2 million tonnes."},{"en":"A similar pattern was seen","context":"In the United Kingdom, road transport produced 32.6 million tonnes of emissions in 2004, 2 million more than ten years earlier, while the other three transport sources showed no remarkable growth. A similar pattern was seen in France, where road transport added 3.6 million tonnes to the total emissions within ten years. Germany, by contrast, was the only country of the three to experience a drop in road transport emissions, and its other transport sources also had a lower emission volume, with the exception of civil aviation, where the amount rose to 1.2 million tonnes."},{"en":"with the exception of civil aviation","context":"In the United Kingdom, road transport produced 32.6 million tonnes of emissions in 2004, 2 million more than ten years earlier, while the other three transport sources showed no remarkable growth. A similar pattern was seen in France, where road transport added 3.6 million tonnes to the total emissions within ten years. Germany, by contrast, was the only country of the three to experience a drop in road transport emissions, and its other transport sources also had a lower emission volume, with the exception of civil aviation, where the amount rose to 1.2 million tonnes."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-039-1"]},"_displayNo":"#013","summary":"Table below shows carbon dioxide emissions from transport in three"},{"id":"writing-academic-059","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Environment & Climate","title":"Global greenhouse gas emissions","prompt":"The charts compare the sources of global greenhouse gas emissions in 2002 and emissions from electricity generation.","answer":"The two pie charts compare the shares of global greenhouse gas emissions produced by three groups of countries in 2002 and 2030, while the bar chart shows projected carbon dioxide emissions from different sectors in the same two years.\n\nOverall, developing countries <strong class=\"key-phrase\">will overtake OECD countries</strong> to become the largest of the three groups in terms of greenhouse gas emissions by 2030. While industry is responsible for the highest amount of these emissions, the consumer and transportation sectors will experience the greatest increases.\n\nAs can be seen in the two pie charts, developing countries are expected to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">account for a bigger share of emissions</strong> in 2030, 48%, compared to 38% in 2002. In contrast, OECD countries are projected to make up a lower proportion, 43%, 9 <strong class=\"key-phrase\">percentage points</strong> lower than in 2002. Countries with transition economies are estimated to take up the remaining 9% in 2030, 1 percentage point less than in 2002.\n\nThe bar chart reveals that carbon dioxide emissions <strong class=\"key-phrase\">will expand to varying degrees</strong> in different sectors by 2030. Industry will continue to be the major producer of carbon dioxide, rising to more than 10 billion tonnes in 2030. Next comes the consumer sector, which is projected to more than double its emissions from less than 4 billion to 8 billion or more tonnes. Transportation, the third largest producer, will see huge growth, to 6 billion tonnes, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">triple the amount in 2002</strong>. By comparison, waste combustion and other sectors <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make up a much lower proportion</strong>, each producing an estimated 1 billion tonnes of carbon dioxide.","phrases":[{"en":"will overtake OECD countries","context":"Overall, developing countries will overtake OECD countries to become the largest of the three groups in terms of greenhouse gas emissions by 2030. While industry is responsible for the highest amount of these emissions, the consumer and transportation sectors will experience the greatest increases."},{"en":"account for a bigger share of emissions","context":"As can be seen in the two pie charts, developing countries are expected to account for a bigger share of emissions in 2030, 48%, compared to 38% in 2002. In contrast, OECD countries are projected to make up a lower proportion, 43%, 9 percentage points lower than in 2002. Countries with transition economies are estimated to take up the remaining 9% in 2030, 1 percentage point less than in 2002."},{"en":"percentage points","context":"As can be seen in the two pie charts, developing countries are expected to account for a bigger share of emissions in 2030, 48%, compared to 38% in 2002. In contrast, OECD countries are projected to make up a lower proportion, 43%, 9 percentage points lower than in 2002. Countries with transition economies are estimated to take up the remaining 9% in 2030, 1 percentage point less than in 2002."},{"en":"will expand to varying degrees","context":"The bar chart reveals that carbon dioxide emissions will expand to varying degrees in different sectors by 2030. Industry will continue to be the major producer of carbon dioxide, rising to more than 10 billion tonnes in 2030. Next comes the consumer sector, which is projected to more than double its emissions from less than 4 billion to 8 billion or more tonnes. Transportation, the third largest producer, will see huge growth, to 6 billion tonnes, triple the amount in 2002. By comparison, waste combustion and other sectors make up a much lower proportion, each producing an estimated 1 billion tonnes of carbon dioxide."},{"en":"triple the amount in 2002","context":"The bar chart reveals that carbon dioxide emissions will expand to varying degrees in different sectors by 2030. Industry will continue to be the major producer of carbon dioxide, rising to more than 10 billion tonnes in 2030. Next comes the consumer sector, which is projected to more than double its emissions from less than 4 billion to 8 billion or more tonnes. Transportation, the third largest producer, will see huge growth, to 6 billion tonnes, triple the amount in 2002. By comparison, waste combustion and other sectors make up a much lower proportion, each producing an estimated 1 billion tonnes of carbon dioxide."},{"en":"make up a much lower proportion","context":"The bar chart reveals that carbon dioxide emissions will expand to varying degrees in different sectors by 2030. Industry will continue to be the major producer of carbon dioxide, rising to more than 10 billion tonnes in 2030. Next comes the consumer sector, which is projected to more than double its emissions from less than 4 billion to 8 billion or more tonnes. Transportation, the third largest producer, will see huge growth, to 6 billion tonnes, triple the amount in 2002. By comparison, waste combustion and other sectors make up a much lower proportion, each producing an estimated 1 billion tonnes of carbon dioxide."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-059-1"]},"_displayNo":"#014","summary":"Pie graphs show greenhouse gas emissions worldwide in 2002 and the"},{"id":"writing-academic-026","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Food & Agriculture","title":"Fairtrade coffee and banana sales","prompt":"The tables below give information about sales of Fairtrade-labelled coffee and bananas in 1999 and 2004 in five European countries.","answer":"The tables show the amount of money spent on Fairtrade coffee and bananas in two separate years in the UK, Switzerland, Denmark, Belgium and Sweden.\n\nIt is clear that sales of Fairtrade coffee rose in all five European countries from 1999 to 2004, but sales of Fairtrade bananas only went up in three out of the five countries. Overall, the UK saw by far <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the highest levels of spending</strong> on the two products.\n\nIn 1999, Switzerland had the highest sales of Fairtrade coffee, at €3 million, while revenue from Fairtrade bananas was highest in the UK, at €15 million. By 2004, however, sales of Fairtrade coffee in the UK had risen to €20 million, and this was more than three times as high as Switzerland&#x27;s sales figure for Fairtrade coffee in that year. The year 2004 also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">saw dramatic increases in</strong> the money spent on Fairtrade bananas in the UK and Switzerland, with revenues rising by €32 million and €4.5 million <strong class=\"key-phrase\">respectively</strong>.\n\nSales of the two Fairtrade products were far lower in Denmark, Belgium and Sweden. Small increases in sales of Fairtrade coffee can be seen, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">revenue remained at €2 million or below</strong> in all three countries in both years. Finally, it is noticeable that the money spent on Fairtrade bananas actually fell in Belgium and Sweden.","phrases":[{"en":"the highest levels of spending","context":"It is clear that sales of Fairtrade coffee rose in all five European countries from 1999 to 2004, but sales of Fairtrade bananas only went up in three out of the five countries. Overall, the UK saw by far the highest levels of spending on the two products."},{"en":"saw dramatic increases in","context":"In 1999, Switzerland had the highest sales of Fairtrade coffee, at €3 million, while revenue from Fairtrade bananas was highest in the UK, at €15 million. By 2004, however, sales of Fairtrade coffee in the UK had risen to €20 million, and this was more than three times as high as Switzerland's sales figure for Fairtrade coffee in that year. The year 2004 also saw dramatic increases in the money spent on Fairtrade bananas in the UK and Switzerland, with revenues rising by €32 million and €4.5 million respectively."},{"en":"respectively","context":"In 1999, Switzerland had the highest sales of Fairtrade coffee, at €3 million, while revenue from Fairtrade bananas was highest in the UK, at €15 million. By 2004, however, sales of Fairtrade coffee in the UK had risen to €20 million, and this was more than three times as high as Switzerland's sales figure for Fairtrade coffee in that year. The year 2004 also saw dramatic increases in the money spent on Fairtrade bananas in the UK and Switzerland, with revenues rising by €32 million and €4.5 million respectively."},{"en":"revenue remained at €2 million or below","context":"Sales of the two Fairtrade products were far lower in Denmark, Belgium and Sweden. Small increases in sales of Fairtrade coffee can be seen, but revenue remained at €2 million or below in all three countries in both years. Finally, it is noticeable that the money spent on Fairtrade bananas actually fell in Belgium and Sweden."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-026-1"]},"_displayNo":"#015","summary":"Tables below give information about sales of Fairtrade*-labelled coffee and…"},{"id":"writing-academic-047","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Food & Agriculture","title":"World food and oil prices","prompt":"The graph below shows changes in global food and oil prices between 2000 and 2011.","answer":"The line graph compares the average price of a barrel of oil with the food price index over a period of 11 years.\n\nIt is clear that average global prices of both oil and food <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rose considerably</strong> between 2000 and 2011. Also, the trends for both commodities were very similar, and so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a strong correlation (93.6%) is suggested</strong>.\n\nIn the year 2000, the average global oil price was close to $25 per barrel, and the food price index stood at just under 90 points. Over the following four years both prices remained relatively stable, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">before rising steadily between 2004 and 2007</strong>. By 2007, the average oil price <strong class=\"key-phrase\">had more than doubled</strong>, to nearly $60 per barrel, and food prices had risen by around 50 points.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">A dramatic increase</strong> in both commodity prices was seen from 2007 to 2008, with oil prices reaching a peak of approximately $130 per barrel and the food price index rising to 220 points. However, by the beginning of 2009 the price of oil had dropped by roughly $90, and the food price index was down by about 80 points. Finally, in 2011, the average oil price rose once again, to nearly $100 per barrel, while the food price index <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reached its peak</strong>, at almost 240 points.","phrases":[{"en":"rose considerably","context":"It is clear that average global prices of both oil and food rose considerably between 2000 and 2011. Also, the trends for both commodities were very similar, and so a strong correlation (93.6%) is suggested."},{"en":"a strong correlation (93.6%) is suggested","context":"It is clear that average global prices of both oil and food rose considerably between 2000 and 2011. Also, the trends for both commodities were very similar, and so a strong correlation (93.6%) is suggested."},{"en":"before rising steadily between 2004 and 2007","context":"In the year 2000, the average global oil price was close to $25 per barrel, and the food price index stood at just under 90 points. Over the following four years both prices remained relatively stable, before rising steadily between 2004 and 2007. By 2007, the average oil price had more than doubled, to nearly $60 per barrel, and food prices had risen by around 50 points."},{"en":"had more than doubled","context":"In the year 2000, the average global oil price was close to $25 per barrel, and the food price index stood at just under 90 points. Over the following four years both prices remained relatively stable, before rising steadily between 2004 and 2007. By 2007, the average oil price had more than doubled, to nearly $60 per barrel, and food prices had risen by around 50 points."},{"en":"A dramatic increase","context":"A dramatic increase in both commodity prices was seen from 2007 to 2008, with oil prices reaching a peak of approximately $130 per barrel and the food price index rising to 220 points. However, by the beginning of 2009 the price of oil had dropped by roughly $90, and the food price index was down by about 80 points. Finally, in 2011, the average oil price rose once again, to nearly $100 per barrel, while the food price index reached its peak, at almost 240 points."},{"en":"reached its peak","context":"A dramatic increase in both commodity prices was seen from 2007 to 2008, with oil prices reaching a peak of approximately $130 per barrel and the food price index rising to 220 points. However, by the beginning of 2009 the price of oil had dropped by roughly $90, and the food price index was down by about 80 points. Finally, in 2011, the average oil price rose once again, to nearly $100 per barrel, while the food price index reached its peak, at almost 240 points."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-047-1"]},"_displayNo":"#016","summary":"Graph below shows changes in global food and oil prices between 2000 and 2011."},{"id":"writing-academic-048","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Food & Agriculture","title":"US meat consumption","prompt":"The graph below shows trends in US meat and poultry consumption.","answer":"The line graph shows changes in the per capita consumption of beef, pork, broilers and turkey in the United States between 1955 and 2012.\n\nIt is noticeable that beef was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">by far the most popular</strong> of the four types of meat for the majority of the 57-year period. However, a considerable rise can be seen in the consumption of broilers, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">with figures eventually surpassing those for</strong> beef.\n\nBetween 1955 and 1976, US beef consumption rose from around 60 to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a peak of</strong> 90 pounds per person per year. During the same period, consumption of broilers also rose, to nearly 30 pounds per person, while the figures for pork fluctuated between 50 and 40 pounds per person. Turkey was by far the least popular meat, with figures below 10 pounds per capita each year.\n\nBy 2012, the amount of beef consumed by the average American <strong class=\"key-phrase\">had plummeted to around 50 pounds</strong>, but the consumption of broilers <strong class=\"key-phrase\">had doubled since the 1970s</strong>, to approximately 55 pounds per capita. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">By contrast</strong>, pork fluctuated and ended slightly lower, at around 43 pounds per person. Turkey <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rose overall</strong> from approximately 5 to 10 pounds, remaining the least consumed of the four meats.","phrases":[{"en":"by far the most popular","context":"It is noticeable that beef was by far the most popular of the four types of meat for the majority of the 57-year period. However, a considerable rise can be seen in the consumption of broilers, with figures eventually surpassing those for beef."},{"en":"with figures eventually surpassing those for","context":"It is noticeable that beef was by far the most popular of the four types of meat for the majority of the 57-year period. However, a considerable rise can be seen in the consumption of broilers, with figures eventually surpassing those for beef."},{"en":"a peak of","context":"Between 1955 and 1976, US beef consumption rose from around 60 to a peak of 90 pounds per person per year. During the same period, consumption of broilers also rose, to nearly 30 pounds per person, while the figures for pork fluctuated between 50 and 40 pounds per person. Turkey was by far the least popular meat, with figures below 10 pounds per capita each year."},{"en":"had plummeted to around 50 pounds","context":"By 2012, the amount of beef consumed by the average American had plummeted to around 50 pounds, but the consumption of broilers had doubled since the 1970s, to approximately 55 pounds per capita. By contrast, pork fluctuated and ended slightly lower, at around 43 pounds per person. Turkey rose overall from approximately 5 to 10 pounds, remaining the least consumed of the four meats."},{"en":"had doubled since the 1970s","context":"By 2012, the amount of beef consumed by the average American had plummeted to around 50 pounds, but the consumption of broilers had doubled since the 1970s, to approximately 55 pounds per capita. By contrast, pork fluctuated and ended slightly lower, at around 43 pounds per person. Turkey rose overall from approximately 5 to 10 pounds, remaining the least consumed of the four meats."},{"en":"By contrast","context":"By 2012, the amount of beef consumed by the average American had plummeted to around 50 pounds, but the consumption of broilers had doubled since the 1970s, to approximately 55 pounds per capita. By contrast, pork fluctuated and ended slightly lower, at around 43 pounds per person. Turkey rose overall from approximately 5 to 10 pounds, remaining the least consumed of the four meats."},{"en":"rose overall","context":"By 2012, the amount of beef consumed by the average American had plummeted to around 50 pounds, but the consumption of broilers had doubled since the 1970s, to approximately 55 pounds per capita. By contrast, pork fluctuated and ended slightly lower, at around 43 pounds per person. Turkey rose overall from approximately 5 to 10 pounds, remaining the least consumed of the four meats."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-048-1"]},"_displayNo":"#017","summary":"Graph below shows trends in US meat and poultry consumption."},{"id":"writing-academic-057","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Food & Agriculture","title":"Fruit production and fertiliser use","prompt":"The first graph below shows the weight (in tonnes) of fruit produced by a farm during each month in 2013, and also the amount (in tonnes) of fertiliser used. The second graph shows the volume of rainfall in each month (in millimetres). Write a report summarising the information. Select and describe the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The charts give information about the tonnage of fruit produced by a farm in each month of 2013, the amount of fertiliser used, and the volume of rainfall in that year.\n\nOverall, the farm generally used the most fertiliser at times of high rainfall, and by contrast it produced the most fruit when both rainfall and fertiliser use were around their lowest.\n\nFruit production rose steadily from just over 2 tonnes in January to a peak of roughly 4.2 tonnes by August. Output then fell rapidly, declining to around the initial 2 tonne mark in December.\n\nFertiliser use <strong class=\"key-phrase\">followed a largely reversed seasonal pattern</strong>. The greatest amount, just under 1 tonne, was used in April, and this was followed by <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a marked decline to zero</strong> in June and July. Fertiliser consumption then increased slightly in the autumn, rising to a total of around 0.7 tonnes <strong class=\"key-phrase\">before dipping to just below 0.5 tonnes</strong> at the end of the year.\n\nRainfall <strong class=\"key-phrase\">appeared to reflect the use of fertiliser</strong>, with an early high of 16mm in February, falling consistently to 4mm in July, August and September. Rainfall then increased steadily to reach 14mm in December.","phrases":[{"en":"followed a largely reversed seasonal pattern","context":"Fertiliser use followed a largely reversed seasonal pattern. The greatest amount, just under 1 tonne, was used in April, and this was followed by a marked decline to zero in June and July. Fertiliser consumption then increased slightly in the autumn, rising to a total of around 0.7 tonnes before dipping to just below 0.5 tonnes at the end of the year."},{"en":"a marked decline to zero","context":"Fertiliser use followed a largely reversed seasonal pattern. The greatest amount, just under 1 tonne, was used in April, and this was followed by a marked decline to zero in June and July. Fertiliser consumption then increased slightly in the autumn, rising to a total of around 0.7 tonnes before dipping to just below 0.5 tonnes at the end of the year."},{"en":"before dipping to just below 0.5 tonnes","context":"Fertiliser use followed a largely reversed seasonal pattern. The greatest amount, just under 1 tonne, was used in April, and this was followed by a marked decline to zero in June and July. Fertiliser consumption then increased slightly in the autumn, rising to a total of around 0.7 tonnes before dipping to just below 0.5 tonnes at the end of the year."},{"en":"appeared to reflect the use of fertiliser","context":"Rainfall appeared to reflect the use of fertiliser, with an early high of 16mm in February, falling consistently to 4mm in July, August and September. Rainfall then increased steadily to reach 14mm in December."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-057-1","writing-academic-057-2"]},"_displayNo":"#018","summary":"First graph below shows the weight (in tonnes) of fruit produced by a farm…"},{"id":"writing-academic-060","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Health","title":"Teen smoking in Britain","prompt":"The table below describes the proportion of smokers in males and females (between the age of 15 and 20) in Britain. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The table shows the proportions of males and females aged between 15 and 20 who were smokers in Britain over a 30-year period.\n\nIt is clear from the table that the 15-to-20-year-old population in Britain had a much higher percentage of smokers by the end of the last century than in the early 1970s.\n\nIn 1970, smoking was not very popular among youngsters aged 15 to 20, with only 10 per cent of males and 5 per cent of females smoking. The following decade saw a dramatic change: the smoking rate among males <strong class=\"key-phrase\">leapt by nearly 13 percentage points</strong> every five years to reach 35% in 1980, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">compared with</strong> slower growth among females, whose rate rose by approximately 8 points every five years to 22% in 1980.\n\nOver the period between 1980 and 1990, smoking remained popular among young males, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accounting for over a third of</strong> the 15-to-20 male population, while the proportion of young females who smoked increased, taking their smoking rate first to 34% in 1985 and then to 37% in 1990, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">even overtaking their male counterparts</strong>.\n\nOver the final years of the last century, smoking became less common in the 15-20 age group, both among males and among females. In 2000, an estimated 27% to 28% of people in this age group were reported to be smokers, lower than in previous years, while both figures remained above their respective 1970 levels.","phrases":[{"en":"leapt by nearly 13 percentage points","context":"In 1970, smoking was not very popular among youngsters aged 15 to 20, with only 10 per cent of males and 5 per cent of females smoking. The following decade saw a dramatic change: the smoking rate among males leapt by nearly 13 percentage points every five years to reach 35% in 1980, compared with slower growth among females, whose rate rose by approximately 8 points every five years to 22% in 1980."},{"en":"compared with","context":"In 1970, smoking was not very popular among youngsters aged 15 to 20, with only 10 per cent of males and 5 per cent of females smoking. The following decade saw a dramatic change: the smoking rate among males leapt by nearly 13 percentage points every five years to reach 35% in 1980, compared with slower growth among females, whose rate rose by approximately 8 points every five years to 22% in 1980."},{"en":"accounting for over a third of","context":"Over the period between 1980 and 1990, smoking remained popular among young males, accounting for over a third of the 15-to-20 male population, while the proportion of young females who smoked increased, taking their smoking rate first to 34% in 1985 and then to 37% in 1990, even overtaking their male counterparts."},{"en":"even overtaking their male counterparts","context":"Over the period between 1980 and 1990, smoking remained popular among young males, accounting for over a third of the 15-to-20 male population, while the proportion of young females who smoked increased, taking their smoking rate first to 34% in 1985 and then to 37% in 1990, even overtaking their male counterparts."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-060-1"]},"_displayNo":"#019","summary":"Table below describes the proportion of smokers in males and females…"},{"id":"writing-academic-062","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Health","title":"Emergency department attendance","prompt":"The chart below shows figures for attendances at hospital emergency care departments in Northern Ireland by age group in December 2016 and December 2017.","answer":"The bar chart compares the rates of attendance in six age categories to hospital emergency wards in Northern Ireland in December 2016 and December 2017.\n\nIt is clear that elderly people and the youngest children were the most frequent visitors to A&amp;E (accident and emergency) departments. Also, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">there was a slight increase in</strong> the rate of emergency care attendances among all six age groups from December 2016 to December 2017.\n\nLooking first at the older age groups, there were roughly 65 A&amp;E attendances <strong class=\"key-phrase\">per 1,000 people</strong> aged 75 and over in December 2016, and this rose to almost 70 attendances per 1000-population one year later. Interestingly, the attendance rate for those aged 65 to 74 was less than half as high, with around 30 attendances per 1000-population in both months.\n\nAt the other end of the age scale, the figures for A&amp;E visits by under-5s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rose marginally</strong> from around 50 to approximately 52 per 1000-population over the one-year period. Children aged 5 to 15 <strong class=\"key-phrase\">made the fewest visits</strong>, at just over 20 per 1000-population in both months. Finally, there was almost no difference in the figures for emergency care attendances among people aged 16 to 44, 45 to 64 and 65 to 74.","phrases":[{"en":"there was a slight increase in","context":"It is clear that elderly people and the youngest children were the most frequent visitors to A&E (accident and emergency) departments. Also, there was a slight increase in the rate of emergency care attendances among all six age groups from December 2016 to December 2017."},{"en":"per 1,000 people","context":"Looking first at the older age groups, there were roughly 65 A&E attendances per 1,000 people aged 75 and over in December 2016, and this rose to almost 70 attendances per 1000-population one year later. Interestingly, the attendance rate for those aged 65 to 74 was less than half as high, with around 30 attendances per 1000-population in both months."},{"en":"rose marginally","context":"At the other end of the age scale, the figures for A&E visits by under-5s rose marginally from around 50 to approximately 52 per 1000-population over the one-year period. Children aged 5 to 15 made the fewest visits, at just over 20 per 1000-population in both months. Finally, there was almost no difference in the figures for emergency care attendances among people aged 16 to 44, 45 to 64 and 65 to 74."},{"en":"made the fewest visits","context":"At the other end of the age scale, the figures for A&E visits by under-5s rose marginally from around 50 to approximately 52 per 1000-population over the one-year period. Children aged 5 to 15 made the fewest visits, at just over 20 per 1000-population in both months. Finally, there was almost no difference in the figures for emergency care attendances among people aged 16 to 44, 45 to 64 and 65 to 74."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-062-1"]},"_displayNo":"#020","summary":"Chart below shows figures for attendances at hospital emergency care…"},{"id":"writing-academic-064","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Health","title":"UK obesity trends","prompt":"The graphs below summarise the results of a survey carried out by the Department of Health about the overweight population in Britain with projections. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The graphs give information about the numbers of adults and children classified as obese in Britain, together with projections up to 2010, and about the link between obesity in children and in their parents.\n\nOverall, the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">projected figures</strong> for 2010 are higher than the 2003 estimates for all four groups. The projections show men outnumbering women and girls outnumbering boys, and children with at least one parent classified as obese are more likely to be obese than those whose parents have a healthy weight.\n\nThe first two graphs show <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a marked rise in obesity</strong> in Britain by 2010. The number of men classified as obese is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">estimated to be as high as</strong> 6.8 million in 2010, over 50% higher than in 2003. The increase among women with the same problem tends to be smaller, rising almost 30% from 4.7 million to 6 million.\n\nThe picture is different for children. The chart suggests that the number of girls classified as obese <strong class=\"key-phrase\">was projected to</strong> increase more during the period 2003-2010, to more than 900 thousand, 100 thousand more than boys with the same problem, although in 2003 there were more boys than girls suffering from obesity, over 700 thousand versus about 690 thousand.\n\nAs shown in the third graph, children in households where both parents are obese are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">twice as likely to be obese as</strong> those in households where one parent is obese (12.5%), and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">five times as likely as</strong> those whose parents have a healthy weight (5%).","phrases":[{"en":"projected figures","context":"Overall, the projected figures for 2010 are higher than the 2003 estimates for all four groups. The projections show men outnumbering women and girls outnumbering boys, and children with at least one parent classified as obese are more likely to be obese than those whose parents have a healthy weight."},{"en":"a marked rise in obesity","context":"The first two graphs show a marked rise in obesity in Britain by 2010. The number of men classified as obese is estimated to be as high as 6.8 million in 2010, over 50% higher than in 2003. The increase among women with the same problem tends to be smaller, rising almost 30% from 4.7 million to 6 million."},{"en":"estimated to be as high as","context":"The first two graphs show a marked rise in obesity in Britain by 2010. The number of men classified as obese is estimated to be as high as 6.8 million in 2010, over 50% higher than in 2003. The increase among women with the same problem tends to be smaller, rising almost 30% from 4.7 million to 6 million."},{"en":"was projected to","context":"The picture is different for children. The chart suggests that the number of girls classified as obese was projected to increase more during the period 2003-2010, to more than 900 thousand, 100 thousand more than boys with the same problem, although in 2003 there were more boys than girls suffering from obesity, over 700 thousand versus about 690 thousand."},{"en":"twice as likely to be obese as","context":"As shown in the third graph, children in households where both parents are obese are twice as likely to be obese as those in households where one parent is obese (12.5%), and five times as likely as those whose parents have a healthy weight (5%)."},{"en":"five times as likely as","context":"As shown in the third graph, children in households where both parents are obese are twice as likely to be obese as those in households where one parent is obese (12.5%), and five times as likely as those whose parents have a healthy weight (5%)."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-064-1","writing-academic-064-2"]},"_displayNo":"#021","summary":"Graphs below summarise the results of a survey carried out by the…"},{"id":"writing-academic-065","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Health","title":"Doctor consultation times","prompt":"The table shows data about the average length of time (in minutes) that people of different ages spend in a consultation with family doctors in a number of countries. Write a report summarising the information. Select and describe the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The table gives information about the typical time that patients spend with their family doctors in a number of countries, broken down by age group.\n\nConsultation times generally rise with age in France, the USA, the UK, Italy and Chile. Brazil and India reach a peak and then fall, while Japan fluctuates and Kenya shows an overall decline.\n\nThe figure for France <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more than doubles</strong>, from 16 to 38 minutes, between the youngest and the oldest people. Likewise, the USA figure rises from 13 to 31, the UK from 9 to 21, and Italy from 17 to 29. These are all substantial increases, while Chile has a consistent but much smaller rise, from 32 to 39 minutes.\n\nBrazil also shows a rise, although after age 60 there is a decline from 26 minutes to an eventual 20. India has <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a more marked rise and fall</strong>, peaking at 21 minutes for people aged 40 to 60 and declining to 11 minutes for those aged 70 and over. In Japan the figures <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fluctuate across the age groups</strong>, between 12 and 16 minutes, while Kenya shows a non-increasing pattern as age rises, from 15 minutes for the youngest to 10 minutes for the oldest people.","phrases":[{"en":"more than doubles","context":"The figure for France more than doubles, from 16 to 38 minutes, between the youngest and the oldest people. Likewise, the USA figure rises from 13 to 31, the UK from 9 to 21, and Italy from 17 to 29. These are all substantial increases, while Chile has a consistent but much smaller rise, from 32 to 39 minutes."},{"en":"a more marked rise and fall","context":"Brazil also shows a rise, although after age 60 there is a decline from 26 minutes to an eventual 20. India has a more marked rise and fall, peaking at 21 minutes for people aged 40 to 60 and declining to 11 minutes for those aged 70 and over. In Japan the figures fluctuate across the age groups, between 12 and 16 minutes, while Kenya shows a non-increasing pattern as age rises, from 15 minutes for the youngest to 10 minutes for the oldest people."},{"en":"fluctuate across the age groups","context":"Brazil also shows a rise, although after age 60 there is a decline from 26 minutes to an eventual 20. India has a more marked rise and fall, peaking at 21 minutes for people aged 40 to 60 and declining to 11 minutes for those aged 70 and over. In Japan the figures fluctuate across the age groups, between 12 and 16 minutes, while Kenya shows a non-increasing pattern as age rises, from 15 minutes for the youngest to 10 minutes for the oldest people."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-065-1"]},"_displayNo":"#022","summary":"Table shows data about the average length of time (in minutes) that people…"},{"id":"writing-academic-001","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Maps & Plans","title":"Chorleywood village development","prompt":"Chorleywood is a village near London whose population has increased steadily since the middle of the nineteenth century. The map below shows the development of the village.","answer":"The map shows the growth of a village called Chorleywood between 1868 and 1994.\n\nIt is clear that the village grew as the transport infrastructure was improved. Four periods of development are shown on the map, and each of the populated areas is near to the main roads, the railway or the motorway.\n\nFrom 1868 to 1883, Chorleywood <strong class=\"key-phrase\">covered a small area</strong> next to one of the main roads. Chorleywood Park and Golf Course is now located next to this original village area. The village <strong class=\"key-phrase\">grew along the main road to the south</strong> between 1883 and 1922, and in 1909 a railway line was built crossing this area from west to east. Chorleywood station is in this part of the village.\n\nThe expansion of Chorleywood continued to the east and west alongside the railway line until 1970. At that time, a motorway was built to the east of the village, and from 1970 to 1994, further development of the village took place <strong class=\"key-phrase\">around motorway intersections</strong> with the railway and one of the main roads.","phrases":[{"en":"covered a small area","context":"From 1868 to 1883, Chorleywood covered a small area next to one of the main roads. Chorleywood Park and Golf Course is now located next to this original village area. The village grew along the main road to the south between 1883 and 1922, and in 1909 a railway line was built crossing this area from west to east. Chorleywood station is in this part of the village."},{"en":"grew along the main road to the south","context":"From 1868 to 1883, Chorleywood covered a small area next to one of the main roads. Chorleywood Park and Golf Course is now located next to this original village area. The village grew along the main road to the south between 1883 and 1922, and in 1909 a railway line was built crossing this area from west to east. Chorleywood station is in this part of the village."},{"en":"around motorway intersections","context":"The expansion of Chorleywood continued to the east and west alongside the railway line until 1970. At that time, a motorway was built to the east of the village, and from 1970 to 1994, further development of the village took place around motorway intersections with the railway and one of the main roads."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-001-1"]},"_displayNo":"#023","summary":"Chorleywood is a village near London whose population has increased…"},{"id":"writing-academic-029","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Maps & Plans","title":"School layout redevelopment","prompt":"The diagrams below show the site of a school in 2004 and the plan for changes to the school site in 2024.","answer":"The two pictures compare the layout of a school as it was in the year 2004 with a proposed site design for the year 2024.\n\nIt is clear that the main change for 2024 would involve the addition of a new school building. The school would then be able to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accommodate a considerably larger number of students</strong>.\n\nIn 2004, there were 600 pupils attending the school, and the two school buildings were <strong class=\"key-phrase\">separated by a path</strong> running from the main entrance to the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sports field</strong>. Under <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the proposed layout</strong> for 2024, the school would have 1,000 pupils and a third building. The plan is also to join the two original buildings together, creating a shorter path that links the buildings only.\n\nAs the third building and a second car park would be built on the site of the original sports field, a new, smaller sports field would need to be laid. A new road would also be built from the main entrance to the second car park. Finally, no changes would be made to the main entrance and original car park.","phrases":[{"en":"accommodate a considerably larger number of students","context":"It is clear that the main change for 2024 would involve the addition of a new school building. The school would then be able to accommodate a considerably larger number of students."},{"en":"separated by a path","context":"In 2004, there were 600 pupils attending the school, and the two school buildings were separated by a path running from the main entrance to the sports field. Under the proposed layout for 2024, the school would have 1,000 pupils and a third building. The plan is also to join the two original buildings together, creating a shorter path that links the buildings only."},{"en":"sports field","context":"In 2004, there were 600 pupils attending the school, and the two school buildings were separated by a path running from the main entrance to the sports field. Under the proposed layout for 2024, the school would have 1,000 pupils and a third building. The plan is also to join the two original buildings together, creating a shorter path that links the buildings only."},{"en":"the proposed layout","context":"In 2004, there were 600 pupils attending the school, and the two school buildings were separated by a path running from the main entrance to the sports field. Under the proposed layout for 2024, the school would have 1,000 pupils and a third building. The plan is also to join the two original buildings together, creating a shorter path that links the buildings only."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-029-1"]},"_displayNo":"#024","summary":"The site of a school in 2004 and the proposed changes for 2024."},{"id":"writing-academic-050","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Maps & Plans","title":"Garlsdon supermarket sites","prompt":"The map below is of the town of Garlsdon and shows two possible sites for a new supermarket. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The map shows two possible sites for a supermarket in Garlsdon, a town with a population of 65,000. One site, S1, is in the countryside to the north-west, while S2 is in the town centre.\n\nOverall, the main contrast is between an out-of-town location beside a main road and a central location within <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a no-traffic zone</strong>. Both sites are close to the railway, and the surrounding towns are connected to Garlsdon by main roads.\n\nS1 lies outside the housing area, near the road towards Hindon, which is 12 kilometres away and has a population of 10,000. The railway passes nearby before continuing through Garlsdon towards the south-east. This proposed site is also north-west of the town&#x27;s industrial and residential areas.\n\nS2 is surrounded by housing and occupies a position within the central no-traffic zone. The railway crosses the northern part of this central area, but the main roads do not run through it. Roads from the town lead south-west towards Bransdon, 16 kilometres away with 15,000 residents, and south-east towards Cransdon, 25 kilometres away with a population of 29,000. The map therefore presents different transport settings for the two locations without identifying a preferred site.","phrases":[{"en":"a no-traffic zone","context":"Overall, the main contrast is between an out-of-town location beside a main road and a central location within a no-traffic zone. Both sites are close to the railway, and the surrounding towns are connected to Garlsdon by main roads."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-050-1"]},"_displayNo":"#025","summary":"Map below is of the town of Garlsdon and shows two possible sites for a new…"},{"id":"writing-academic-019","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Money & Spending","title":"Youth spending and life milestones","prompt":"Charts 1 and 2 below show the proportion of income spent on different areas by young people in Canada and Poland. Chart 3 shows the average age of certain life events for these countries, and an estimated global average age. Write a report summarising the information. Select and describe the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The charts show the percentage of youth income spent on various areas in Poland and Canada, and also the average age at which young people in these countries reach three key life events, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">compared with</strong> an estimated global norm.\n\nOverall, young people&#x27;s expenditure in Canada is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more evenly split</strong> than in Poland, where studies and transport account for around two-thirds of expenditure. In terms of life events, Poland is closer to the global average than Canada.\n\nLooking first at income, in Canada the two largest areas of expense are leisure and clothing, at 20% and 19% <strong class=\"key-phrase\">respectively</strong>. The other areas (housing, food, transport and studies) are all roughly similar, at between 14% and 16%. In Poland, however, studies <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take almost half the expenditure</strong>, at 43%, followed by transport at 26%. Leisure and clothing are similar at 10% and 11%, and housing and food are the same at 5%.\n\nRegarding life events, in Poland leaving home and buying a car happen at an age close to the estimated global average of around 26 years, whereas in Canada they happen much earlier, at 20 years or just under. Getting married, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">by contrast</strong>, happens at a similar age in both countries, at around 30 years, which is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">much later than the world average</strong> of just over 20.","phrases":[{"en":"compared with","context":"The charts show the percentage of youth income spent on various areas in Poland and Canada, and also the average age at which young people in these countries reach three key life events, compared with an estimated global norm."},{"en":"more evenly split","context":"Overall, young people's expenditure in Canada is more evenly split than in Poland, where studies and transport account for around two-thirds of expenditure. In terms of life events, Poland is closer to the global average than Canada."},{"en":"respectively","context":"Looking first at income, in Canada the two largest areas of expense are leisure and clothing, at 20% and 19% respectively. The other areas (housing, food, transport and studies) are all roughly similar, at between 14% and 16%. In Poland, however, studies take almost half the expenditure, at 43%, followed by transport at 26%. Leisure and clothing are similar at 10% and 11%, and housing and food are the same at 5%."},{"en":"take almost half the expenditure","context":"Looking first at income, in Canada the two largest areas of expense are leisure and clothing, at 20% and 19% respectively. The other areas (housing, food, transport and studies) are all roughly similar, at between 14% and 16%. In Poland, however, studies take almost half the expenditure, at 43%, followed by transport at 26%. Leisure and clothing are similar at 10% and 11%, and housing and food are the same at 5%."},{"en":"by contrast","context":"Regarding life events, in Poland leaving home and buying a car happen at an age close to the estimated global average of around 26 years, whereas in Canada they happen much earlier, at 20 years or just under. Getting married, by contrast, happens at a similar age in both countries, at around 30 years, which is much later than the world average of just over 20."},{"en":"much later than the world average","context":"Regarding life events, in Poland leaving home and buying a car happen at an age close to the estimated global average of around 26 years, whereas in Canada they happen much earlier, at 20 years or just under. Getting married, by contrast, happens at a similar age in both countries, at around 30 years, which is much later than the world average of just over 20."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-019-1","writing-academic-019-2"]},"_displayNo":"#026","summary":"Charts 1 and 2 below show the proportion of income spent on different areas…"},{"id":"writing-academic-021","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Money & Spending","title":"World spending and population","prompt":"The charts below give information about world spending and population.\nSummarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The first pie chart compares the shares of four major items in global expenditure, while the second shows how global population <strong class=\"key-phrase\">is distributed</strong> between the continents.\n\nOverall, these four items are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">responsible for three-fifths of the overall spending</strong> around the world, the most prominent share being that of food. The largest share of the world&#x27;s population lives in Asia, which accounts for 57% of the total.\n\nFood takes almost a quarter of global expenditure, followed by transport, at almost 20%. Housing also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accounts for a considerable proportion</strong>, while the smallest of the four shares goes on clothing, at only 6%. The remaining 40% is spent on a variety of other items.\n\nAccording to the second chart, 57% of the population lives in Asia. Europe and the Americas share similar proportions and together are host to 28% of the world&#x27;s population, while the inhabitants of Africa form one-tenth of the total. The remaining 5% live in other regions.","phrases":[{"en":"is distributed","context":"The first pie chart compares the shares of four major items in global expenditure, while the second shows how global population is distributed between the continents."},{"en":"responsible for three-fifths of the overall spending","context":"Overall, these four items are responsible for three-fifths of the overall spending around the world, the most prominent share being that of food. The largest share of the world's population lives in Asia, which accounts for 57% of the total."},{"en":"accounts for a considerable proportion","context":"Food takes almost a quarter of global expenditure, followed by transport, at almost 20%. Housing also accounts for a considerable proportion, while the smallest of the four shares goes on clothing, at only 6%. The remaining 40% is spent on a variety of other items."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-021-1"]},"_displayNo":"#027","summary":"Charts below give information about world spending and population."},{"id":"writing-academic-053","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Money & Spending","title":"Consumer spending by country","prompt":"The table compares consumer spending on food, clothing and leisure in five countries in 2002.","answer":"The table shows percentages of consumer expenditure for three categories of products and services in five countries in 2002.\n\nIt is clear that the largest proportion of consumer spending in each country <strong class=\"key-phrase\">went on food, drinks and tobacco</strong>. On the other hand, the leisure/education category <strong class=\"key-phrase\">has the lowest percentages in the table</strong>.\n\nOut of the five countries, consumer spending on food, drinks and tobacco was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">noticeably higher</strong> in Turkey, at 32.14%, and Ireland, at nearly 29%. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">The proportion of spending on</strong> leisure and education was also highest in Turkey, at 4.35%, while expenditure on clothing and footwear was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">significantly higher in Italy</strong>, at 9%, than in any of the other countries.\n\nIt can be seen that Sweden had the lowest percentages of national consumer expenditure for food/drinks/tobacco and for clothing/footwear, at nearly 16% and just over 5% respectively. Spain had slightly higher figures for these categories, but the lowest figure for leisure/education, at only 1.98%.","phrases":[{"en":"went on food, drinks and tobacco","context":"It is clear that the largest proportion of consumer spending in each country went on food, drinks and tobacco. On the other hand, the leisure/education category has the lowest percentages in the table."},{"en":"has the lowest percentages in the table","context":"It is clear that the largest proportion of consumer spending in each country went on food, drinks and tobacco. On the other hand, the leisure/education category has the lowest percentages in the table."},{"en":"noticeably higher","context":"Out of the five countries, consumer spending on food, drinks and tobacco was noticeably higher in Turkey, at 32.14%, and Ireland, at nearly 29%. The proportion of spending on leisure and education was also highest in Turkey, at 4.35%, while expenditure on clothing and footwear was significantly higher in Italy, at 9%, than in any of the other countries."},{"en":"The proportion of spending on","context":"Out of the five countries, consumer spending on food, drinks and tobacco was noticeably higher in Turkey, at 32.14%, and Ireland, at nearly 29%. The proportion of spending on leisure and education was also highest in Turkey, at 4.35%, while expenditure on clothing and footwear was significantly higher in Italy, at 9%, than in any of the other countries."},{"en":"significantly higher in Italy","context":"Out of the five countries, consumer spending on food, drinks and tobacco was noticeably higher in Turkey, at 32.14%, and Ireland, at nearly 29%. The proportion of spending on leisure and education was also highest in Turkey, at 4.35%, while expenditure on clothing and footwear was significantly higher in Italy, at 9%, than in any of the other countries."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-053-1"]},"_displayNo":"#028","summary":"Table below gives information on consumer spending on different items in…"},{"id":"writing-academic-055","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Money & Spending","title":"Australian family poverty","prompt":"The table shows the proportion of different family types living in poverty in Australia in 1999.","answer":"The table gives information about poverty rates among six types of household in Australia in the year 1999.\n\nIt is noticeable that levels of poverty were higher for single people than for couples, and people with children were more likely to be poor than those without. Poverty rates were considerably lower among elderly people.\n\nOverall, 11% of Australians, or 1,837,000 people, were living in poverty in 1999. Aged people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">were the least likely to be poor</strong>, with poverty levels of 6% and 4% for single aged people and aged couples respectively, the lowest rates of all.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">Just over one fifth of single parents</strong> were living in poverty, the highest rate of any of the six household types, whereas only 12% of parents living with a partner were classed as poor. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">The same pattern can be seen</strong> for people with no children: while 19% of single people in this group were <strong class=\"key-phrase\">living below the poverty line</strong>, the figure for couples was much lower, at only 7%.","phrases":[{"en":"were the least likely to be poor","context":"Overall, 11% of Australians, or 1,837,000 people, were living in poverty in 1999. Aged people were the least likely to be poor, with poverty levels of 6% and 4% for single aged people and aged couples respectively, the lowest rates of all."},{"en":"Just over one fifth of single parents","context":"Just over one fifth of single parents were living in poverty, the highest rate of any of the six household types, whereas only 12% of parents living with a partner were classed as poor. The same pattern can be seen for people with no children: while 19% of single people in this group were living below the poverty line, the figure for couples was much lower, at only 7%."},{"en":"The same pattern can be seen","context":"Just over one fifth of single parents were living in poverty, the highest rate of any of the six household types, whereas only 12% of parents living with a partner were classed as poor. The same pattern can be seen for people with no children: while 19% of single people in this group were living below the poverty line, the figure for couples was much lower, at only 7%."},{"en":"living below the poverty line","context":"Just over one fifth of single parents were living in poverty, the highest rate of any of the six household types, whereas only 12% of parents living with a partner were classed as poor. The same pattern can be seen for people with no children: while 19% of single people in this group were living below the poverty line, the figure for couples was much lower, at only 7%."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-055-1"]},"_displayNo":"#029","summary":"Table below shows the proportion of different categories of families living…"},{"id":"writing-academic-058","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Money & Spending","title":"Dubai gold sales","prompt":"The graph below gives information about Dubai gold sales in 2002.\nSummarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The diagram given illustrates how gold sales in Dubai fluctuated over a period of 12 months. Overall, sales ended 2002 close to their starting level despite substantial fluctuations during the year.\n\nIt is observed that in the first month of 2002, gold sales stood at 200 million dirhams and rose slightly to reach about 225 million in February. This was followed by another increase, although much steeper, in March when sales were almost 125 million dirhams higher than February. However, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">this upward trend was suddenly broken</strong> and sales <strong class=\"key-phrase\">plummeted dramatically</strong> over the next 4 months to reach a little over 100 million dirhams in July. August sales showed a significant rise back to January levels as figures nearly doubled, but this was not to last as they dropped again in September to the same level as they were in July. October brought an increase of about 100 million dirhams in sales, after which sales figures <strong class=\"key-phrase\">levelled off</strong> over the last two months of 2002.\n\nTo sum up, sales were at their highest in March while the weakest sales figures could be observed in July and September.","phrases":[{"en":"this upward trend was suddenly broken","context":"It is observed that in the first month of 2002, gold sales stood at 200 million dirhams and rose slightly to reach about 225 million in February. This was followed by another increase, although much steeper, in March when sales were almost 125 million dirhams higher than February. However, this upward trend was suddenly broken and sales plummeted dramatically over the next 4 months to reach a little over 100 million dirhams in July. August sales showed a significant rise back to January levels as figures nearly doubled, but this was not to last as they dropped again in September to the same level as they were in July. October brought an increase of about 100 million dirhams in sales, after which sales figures levelled off over the last two months of 2002."},{"en":"plummeted dramatically","context":"It is observed that in the first month of 2002, gold sales stood at 200 million dirhams and rose slightly to reach about 225 million in February. This was followed by another increase, although much steeper, in March when sales were almost 125 million dirhams higher than February. However, this upward trend was suddenly broken and sales plummeted dramatically over the next 4 months to reach a little over 100 million dirhams in July. August sales showed a significant rise back to January levels as figures nearly doubled, but this was not to last as they dropped again in September to the same level as they were in July. October brought an increase of about 100 million dirhams in sales, after which sales figures levelled off over the last two months of 2002."},{"en":"levelled off","context":"It is observed that in the first month of 2002, gold sales stood at 200 million dirhams and rose slightly to reach about 225 million in February. This was followed by another increase, although much steeper, in March when sales were almost 125 million dirhams higher than February. However, this upward trend was suddenly broken and sales plummeted dramatically over the next 4 months to reach a little over 100 million dirhams in July. August sales showed a significant rise back to January levels as figures nearly doubled, but this was not to last as they dropped again in September to the same level as they were in July. October brought an increase of about 100 million dirhams in sales, after which sales figures levelled off over the last two months of 2002."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-058-1"]},"_displayNo":"#030","summary":"Graph below gives information about Dubai gold sales in 2002."},{"id":"writing-academic-066","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Money & Spending","title":"Tablet prices and discounts","prompt":"Chart 1 below shows the normal price of a tablet computer in different stores (in US dollars), and the % discount offered by these stores when the products are sold on promotion. Chart 2 shows the percentage increase in volume sales when the discount promotions are happening, and Chart 3 shows funds (in 000 US dollars) spent on TV advertising during these promotions. Write a report summarising the information. Select and describe the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The charts show average prices, promotional discounts, sales uplifts and advertising budgets for seven stores selling tablet computers.\n\nFour of these stores have a normal price of around $300 per item (Slaker, Jones, Scots and Fraser). Two stores (Lewis and Nova) have a much higher price at $460 and $565 respectively, and the lowest price ($150) is seen in Smith. We see <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a similar grouping</strong> in the promotional discounts offered, with the four mid-priced stores offering 10% off, the two higher-priced stores giving 20% (Lewis) and 25% (Nova), and Smith offering only 5% discount.\n\nLewis and Nova also see <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the highest rate of uplift</strong> in sales volumes on promotion, at 21% and 23% respectively, while Slaker, Jones, Scots and Fraser are again similarly grouped with around a 12% increase each. Smith has the smallest sales uplift, at below 5%.\n\nTurning to TV advertising, Slaker, Jones, Scots and Fraser have roughly similar budgets of between $63,000 and $70,000. Lewis and Nova have much lower spends at $18,000 and $21,000, while Smith spends nothing at all.\n\nIn summary, the four mid-priced stores spend the most on advertising and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">achieve similar uplifts with their 10% discounts</strong>. Lewis and Nova spend about one-third of these levels, but achieve <strong class=\"key-phrase\">roughly double the uplift</strong> by offering approximately double the level of discount. Smith, with its lower normal price, is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">barely active on promotions</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"a similar grouping","context":"Four of these stores have a normal price of around $300 per item (Slaker, Jones, Scots and Fraser). Two stores (Lewis and Nova) have a much higher price at $460 and $565 respectively, and the lowest price ($150) is seen in Smith. We see a similar grouping in the promotional discounts offered, with the four mid-priced stores offering 10% off, the two higher-priced stores giving 20% (Lewis) and 25% (Nova), and Smith offering only 5% discount."},{"en":"the highest rate of uplift","context":"Lewis and Nova also see the highest rate of uplift in sales volumes on promotion, at 21% and 23% respectively, while Slaker, Jones, Scots and Fraser are again similarly grouped with around a 12% increase each. Smith has the smallest sales uplift, at below 5%."},{"en":"achieve similar uplifts with their 10% discounts","context":"In summary, the four mid-priced stores spend the most on advertising and achieve similar uplifts with their 10% discounts. Lewis and Nova spend about one-third of these levels, but achieve roughly double the uplift by offering approximately double the level of discount. Smith, with its lower normal price, is barely active on promotions."},{"en":"roughly double the uplift","context":"In summary, the four mid-priced stores spend the most on advertising and achieve similar uplifts with their 10% discounts. Lewis and Nova spend about one-third of these levels, but achieve roughly double the uplift by offering approximately double the level of discount. Smith, with its lower normal price, is barely active on promotions."},{"en":"barely active on promotions","context":"In summary, the four mid-priced stores spend the most on advertising and achieve similar uplifts with their 10% discounts. Lewis and Nova spend about one-third of these levels, but achieve roughly double the uplift by offering approximately double the level of discount. Smith, with its lower normal price, is barely active on promotions."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-066-1","writing-academic-066-2"]},"_displayNo":"#031","summary":"Chart 1 below shows the normal price of a tablet computer in different…"},{"id":"writing-academic-003","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Population & Demographics","title":"Ageing populations","prompt":"The graph below shows the proportion of the population aged 65 and over between 1940 and 2040 in three different countries.","answer":"The line graph compares the percentage of people aged 65 or more in three countries over a period of 100 years.\n\nIt is clear that the proportion of elderly people increases in each country between 1940 and 2040. Japan is expected to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">see the most dramatic changes in</strong> its elderly population.\n\nIn 1940, around 9% of Americans were aged 65 or over, compared to about 7% of Swedish people and 5% of Japanese people. The proportions of elderly people in the USA and Sweden <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rose gradually over the next 50 years</strong>, reaching just under 15% in 1990. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">By contrast</strong>, the figures for Japan <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remained below 5% until the early 2000s</strong>.\n\nLooking into the future, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a sudden increase in</strong> the percentage of elderly people is predicted for Japan, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">with a jump of</strong> over 15 <strong class=\"key-phrase\">percentage points</strong> in just 10 years from 2030 to 2040. By 2040, it is thought that around 27% of the Japanese population will be 65 years old or more, while the figures for Sweden and the USA will be slightly lower, at about 25% and 23% respectively.","phrases":[{"en":"see the most dramatic changes in","context":"It is clear that the proportion of elderly people increases in each country between 1940 and 2040. Japan is expected to see the most dramatic changes in its elderly population."},{"en":"rose gradually over the next 50 years","context":"In 1940, around 9% of Americans were aged 65 or over, compared to about 7% of Swedish people and 5% of Japanese people. The proportions of elderly people in the USA and Sweden rose gradually over the next 50 years, reaching just under 15% in 1990. By contrast, the figures for Japan remained below 5% until the early 2000s."},{"en":"By contrast","context":"In 1940, around 9% of Americans were aged 65 or over, compared to about 7% of Swedish people and 5% of Japanese people. The proportions of elderly people in the USA and Sweden rose gradually over the next 50 years, reaching just under 15% in 1990. By contrast, the figures for Japan remained below 5% until the early 2000s."},{"en":"remained below 5% until the early 2000s","context":"In 1940, around 9% of Americans were aged 65 or over, compared to about 7% of Swedish people and 5% of Japanese people. The proportions of elderly people in the USA and Sweden rose gradually over the next 50 years, reaching just under 15% in 1990. By contrast, the figures for Japan remained below 5% until the early 2000s."},{"en":"a sudden increase in","context":"Looking into the future, a sudden increase in the percentage of elderly people is predicted for Japan, with a jump of over 15 percentage points in just 10 years from 2030 to 2040. By 2040, it is thought that around 27% of the Japanese population will be 65 years old or more, while the figures for Sweden and the USA will be slightly lower, at about 25% and 23% respectively."},{"en":"with a jump of","context":"Looking into the future, a sudden increase in the percentage of elderly people is predicted for Japan, with a jump of over 15 percentage points in just 10 years from 2030 to 2040. By 2040, it is thought that around 27% of the Japanese population will be 65 years old or more, while the figures for Sweden and the USA will be slightly lower, at about 25% and 23% respectively."},{"en":"percentage points","context":"Looking into the future, a sudden increase in the percentage of elderly people is predicted for Japan, with a jump of over 15 percentage points in just 10 years from 2030 to 2040. By 2040, it is thought that around 27% of the Japanese population will be 65 years old or more, while the figures for Sweden and the USA will be slightly lower, at about 25% and 23% respectively."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-003-1"]},"_displayNo":"#032","summary":"Graph below shows the proportion of the population aged 65 and over between…"},{"id":"writing-academic-005","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Population & Demographics","title":"Living alone in England","prompt":"The bar chart below shows the proportions of English men and women of different ages who were living alone in 2011. The pie chart compares the numbers of bedrooms in these one-person households.","answer":"The two charts give information about single-occupant households in England in the year 2011. The bar chart compares figures for occupants&#x27; age and gender, and the pie chart shows data about the number of bedrooms in these homes.\n\nOverall, females made up <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a higher proportion of</strong> people living alone than males, and this difference is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">particularly noticeable</strong> in the older age categories. We can also see that the most common number of bedrooms in a single-occupant home was two.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">A significant majority of</strong> the people aged 65 or over who were living alone in England in 2011 were female. Women made up around 72% of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">single occupants</strong> aged 75 to 84, and 76% of those aged 85 or over. By contrast, among younger adults the figures for males were higher. For example, in the 35-49 age category, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">men accounted for nearly 65%</strong> of people living alone.\n\nIn the same year, 35.4% of one-person households in England had two bedrooms, while one-bedroom and three-bedroom homes accounted for 28% and 29.8% of the total. Under 7% of single-occupant homes had four or more bedrooms.","phrases":[{"en":"a higher proportion of","context":"Overall, females made up a higher proportion of people living alone than males, and this difference is particularly noticeable in the older age categories. We can also see that the most common number of bedrooms in a single-occupant home was two."},{"en":"particularly noticeable","context":"Overall, females made up a higher proportion of people living alone than males, and this difference is particularly noticeable in the older age categories. We can also see that the most common number of bedrooms in a single-occupant home was two."},{"en":"A significant majority of","context":"A significant majority of the people aged 65 or over who were living alone in England in 2011 were female. Women made up around 72% of single occupants aged 75 to 84, and 76% of those aged 85 or over. By contrast, among younger adults the figures for males were higher. For example, in the 35-49 age category, men accounted for nearly 65% of people living alone."},{"en":"single occupants","context":"A significant majority of the people aged 65 or over who were living alone in England in 2011 were female. Women made up around 72% of single occupants aged 75 to 84, and 76% of those aged 85 or over. By contrast, among younger adults the figures for males were higher. For example, in the 35-49 age category, men accounted for nearly 65% of people living alone."},{"en":"men accounted for nearly 65%","context":"A significant majority of the people aged 65 or over who were living alone in England in 2011 were female. Women made up around 72% of single occupants aged 75 to 84, and 76% of those aged 85 or over. By contrast, among younger adults the figures for males were higher. For example, in the 35-49 age category, men accounted for nearly 65% of people living alone."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-005-1"]},"_displayNo":"#033","summary":"Bar chart below shows the proportions of English men and women of different…"},{"id":"writing-academic-012","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Population & Demographics","title":"French and Indian age structures","prompt":"The charts below compare the age structure of the population of France and India in 1984.","answer":"The two charts compare the populations of France and India in terms of age distribution by gender in the year 1984.\n\nIt is clear that the population of India was younger than that of France in 1984, with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a noticeably larger proportion</strong> of people aged under 20. France, on the other hand, had a significantly larger percentage of elderly inhabitants.\n\nIn India, close to 14% of people were aged under 5, and each five-year age bracket above this contained <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an increasingly smaller proportion of the population</strong>. France&#x27;s population, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">by contrast</strong>, was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more evenly distributed</strong> across the age ranges, with similar figures (around 7% to 8% of all people) for each five-year cohort between the ages of 0 and 40. Somewhere between 10% and 15% of all French people were aged 70 or older, but the equivalent figure for India was only 2%.\n\nLooking more closely at gender, there was a noticeably higher proportion of French women than men in every cohort from age 50 upwards. For example, women aged 70 to 74 <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accounted for</strong> almost 3% of the total French population, while men in the same age group represented just under 2%. No significant gender differences can be seen on the Indian population chart.","phrases":[{"en":"a noticeably larger proportion","context":"It is clear that the population of India was younger than that of France in 1984, with a noticeably larger proportion of people aged under 20. France, on the other hand, had a significantly larger percentage of elderly inhabitants."},{"en":"an increasingly smaller proportion of the population","context":"In India, close to 14% of people were aged under 5, and each five-year age bracket above this contained an increasingly smaller proportion of the population. France's population, by contrast, was more evenly distributed across the age ranges, with similar figures (around 7% to 8% of all people) for each five-year cohort between the ages of 0 and 40. Somewhere between 10% and 15% of all French people were aged 70 or older, but the equivalent figure for India was only 2%."},{"en":"by contrast","context":"In India, close to 14% of people were aged under 5, and each five-year age bracket above this contained an increasingly smaller proportion of the population. France's population, by contrast, was more evenly distributed across the age ranges, with similar figures (around 7% to 8% of all people) for each five-year cohort between the ages of 0 and 40. Somewhere between 10% and 15% of all French people were aged 70 or older, but the equivalent figure for India was only 2%."},{"en":"more evenly distributed","context":"In India, close to 14% of people were aged under 5, and each five-year age bracket above this contained an increasingly smaller proportion of the population. France's population, by contrast, was more evenly distributed across the age ranges, with similar figures (around 7% to 8% of all people) for each five-year cohort between the ages of 0 and 40. Somewhere between 10% and 15% of all French people were aged 70 or older, but the equivalent figure for India was only 2%."},{"en":"accounted for","context":"Looking more closely at gender, there was a noticeably higher proportion of French women than men in every cohort from age 50 upwards. For example, women aged 70 to 74 accounted for almost 3% of the total French population, while men in the same age group represented just under 2%. No significant gender differences can be seen on the Indian population chart."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-012-1"]},"_displayNo":"#034","summary":"Charts below compare the age structure of the population of France and…"},{"id":"writing-academic-013","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Population & Demographics","title":"Yemen and Italy populations","prompt":"The charts show the age structure of the populations of Yemen and Italy in 2000 and projections for 2050.","answer":"The pie charts compare the proportions of people falling into three distinct age groups in Yemen and Italy in two different years.\n\nIt is clear that Italy <strong class=\"key-phrase\">had the older population</strong> in the year 2000, and that the same is predicted for the year 2050. The populations of both countries are expected to age over the fifty-year period.\n\nIn the year 2000, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">just over half of</strong> the population of Yemen was aged 14 or under, while most Italians (61.6%) fell into the 15 to 59 age group, and only 14.3% were children under 15 years of age. People aged 60 or over <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accounted for</strong> almost a quarter of the Italian population, but only 3.6% of the inhabitants of Yemen.\n\nBy 2050, the proportion of children under 15 is predicted to drop in both countries, most noticeably in Yemen where the figure is expected to fall by 13.1 <strong class=\"key-phrase\">percentage points</strong>. On the other hand, the figures for elderly people are expected to rise, by 2.1 percentage points in Yemen and a massive 18.2 percentage points in Italy. Finally, it is anticipated that the 15 to 59 age group will grow by around 10 percentage points in Yemen, but shrink by around 15 percentage points in Italy.","phrases":[{"en":"had the older population","context":"It is clear that Italy had the older population in the year 2000, and that the same is predicted for the year 2050. The populations of both countries are expected to age over the fifty-year period."},{"en":"just over half of","context":"In the year 2000, just over half of the population of Yemen was aged 14 or under, while most Italians (61.6%) fell into the 15 to 59 age group, and only 14.3% were children under 15 years of age. People aged 60 or over accounted for almost a quarter of the Italian population, but only 3.6% of the inhabitants of Yemen."},{"en":"accounted for","context":"In the year 2000, just over half of the population of Yemen was aged 14 or under, while most Italians (61.6%) fell into the 15 to 59 age group, and only 14.3% were children under 15 years of age. People aged 60 or over accounted for almost a quarter of the Italian population, but only 3.6% of the inhabitants of Yemen."},{"en":"percentage points","context":"By 2050, the proportion of children under 15 is predicted to drop in both countries, most noticeably in Yemen where the figure is expected to fall by 13.1 percentage points. On the other hand, the figures for elderly people are expected to rise, by 2.1 percentage points in Yemen and a massive 18.2 percentage points in Italy. Finally, it is anticipated that the 15 to 59 age group will grow by around 10 percentage points in Yemen, but shrink by around 15 percentage points in Italy."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-013-1"]},"_displayNo":"#035","summary":"Charts below give information on the ages of the populations of Yemen and…"},{"id":"writing-academic-068","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Population & Demographics","title":"US population demographics","prompt":"Graphs below provide the latest figures about the demographics of the United States, including ethnic groups and the population, based on a census that was carried out and administered by US Census Bureau in 2006. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The table and bar chart describe changes in the size and ethnic composition of the United States population. Overall, the population rose substantially, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the most striking proportional increases</strong> were seen among the Hispanic and Asian communities.\n\nThe total population increased from 200 million in 1966 to 300 million in 2006. White Americans <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remained the largest group</strong>, representing roughly two thirds of the population in 2006, although this group grew more slowly than the population as a whole. The Hispanic population climbed from about 8 million to 44.7 million, while the Asian population <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rose tenfold to 14.3 million</strong>. African Americans, who had been the second-largest group in 1966, became the third-largest by 2006.\n\nThe bar chart also shows that population <strong class=\"key-phrase\">growth accelerated over time</strong>. In the early decades of the twentieth century, the increase varied from around 7.5 million to 20 million per decade. From the middle of the century onwards, the country added at least 20 million people in most ten-year periods, and the rise exceeded 30 million during the 1990s.","phrases":[{"en":"the most striking proportional increases","context":"The table and bar chart describe changes in the size and ethnic composition of the United States population. Overall, the population rose substantially, and the most striking proportional increases were seen among the Hispanic and Asian communities."},{"en":"remained the largest group","context":"The total population increased from 200 million in 1966 to 300 million in 2006. White Americans remained the largest group, representing roughly two thirds of the population in 2006, although this group grew more slowly than the population as a whole. The Hispanic population climbed from about 8 million to 44.7 million, while the Asian population rose tenfold to 14.3 million. African Americans, who had been the second-largest group in 1966, became the third-largest by 2006."},{"en":"rose tenfold to 14.3 million","context":"The total population increased from 200 million in 1966 to 300 million in 2006. White Americans remained the largest group, representing roughly two thirds of the population in 2006, although this group grew more slowly than the population as a whole. The Hispanic population climbed from about 8 million to 44.7 million, while the Asian population rose tenfold to 14.3 million. African Americans, who had been the second-largest group in 1966, became the third-largest by 2006."},{"en":"growth accelerated over time","context":"The bar chart also shows that population growth accelerated over time. In the early decades of the twentieth century, the increase varied from around 7.5 million to 20 million per decade. From the middle of the century onwards, the country added at least 20 million people in most ten-year periods, and the rise exceeded 30 million during the 1990s."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-068-1"]},"_displayNo":"#036","summary":"Graphs below provide the latest figures about the demographics of the…"},{"id":"writing-academic-032","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Processes & Cycles","title":"Waste paper recycling","prompt":"The chart below shows the process of waste paper recycling. The flow chart shows how waste paper is recycled.","answer":"The flow chart illustrates the way in which waste paper is recycled. It is clear that there are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">six distinct stages in this process</strong>, from the initial collection of waste paper to the eventual production of usable paper.\n\nAt the first stage in the paper recycling process, waste paper is collected either from paper banks, where members of the public leave their used paper, or directly from businesses. This paper is then sorted by hand and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">separated according to</strong> its grade, with any paper that is not <strong class=\"key-phrase\">suitable for recycling</strong> being removed. Next, the graded paper <strong class=\"key-phrase\">is transported to</strong> a paper mill.\n\nStages four and five of the process both involve cleaning. The paper is cleaned and pulped, and foreign objects such as staples are taken out. Following this, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">all remnants of ink and glue are removed</strong> from the paper at the de-inking stage. Finally, the pulp can be processed in a paper making machine, which makes the end product: usable paper.","phrases":[{"en":"six distinct stages in this process","context":"The flow chart illustrates the way in which waste paper is recycled. It is clear that there are six distinct stages in this process, from the initial collection of waste paper to the eventual production of usable paper."},{"en":"separated according to","context":"At the first stage in the paper recycling process, waste paper is collected either from paper banks, where members of the public leave their used paper, or directly from businesses. This paper is then sorted by hand and separated according to its grade, with any paper that is not suitable for recycling being removed. Next, the graded paper is transported to a paper mill."},{"en":"suitable for recycling","context":"At the first stage in the paper recycling process, waste paper is collected either from paper banks, where members of the public leave their used paper, or directly from businesses. This paper is then sorted by hand and separated according to its grade, with any paper that is not suitable for recycling being removed. Next, the graded paper is transported to a paper mill."},{"en":"is transported to","context":"At the first stage in the paper recycling process, waste paper is collected either from paper banks, where members of the public leave their used paper, or directly from businesses. This paper is then sorted by hand and separated according to its grade, with any paper that is not suitable for recycling being removed. Next, the graded paper is transported to a paper mill."},{"en":"all remnants of ink and glue are removed","context":"Stages four and five of the process both involve cleaning. The paper is cleaned and pulped, and foreign objects such as staples are taken out. Following this, all remnants of ink and glue are removed from the paper at the de-inking stage. Finally, the pulp can be processed in a paper making machine, which makes the end product: usable paper."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-032-1"]},"_displayNo":"#037","summary":"Chart below shows the process of waste paper recycling."},{"id":"writing-academic-033","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Processes & Cycles","title":"The water cycle","prompt":"The diagram below shows the water cycle, which is the continuous movement of water on, above and below the surface of the Earth.","answer":"The picture illustrates the way in which water passes from ocean to air to land during the natural process known as the water cycle.\n\nThree main stages are shown on the diagram. Ocean water evaporates, falls as rain, and eventually runs back into the oceans again.\n\nBeginning at the evaporation stage, we can see that about 60% of water vapour in the air comes from the oceans. Heat from the sun causes water to evaporate, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">water vapour condenses to form clouds</strong>. At the second stage, labelled ‘precipitation’ on the diagram, water falls as rain or snow.\n\nAt the third stage in the cycle, rainwater <strong class=\"key-phrase\">may take various paths</strong>. Some of it may fall into lakes or return to the oceans via ‘surface runoff’. Otherwise, rainwater may <strong class=\"key-phrase\">filter through the ground</strong>, reaching <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the impervious layer of the earth</strong>. Salt water intrusion is shown to take place just before groundwater passes into the oceans <strong class=\"key-phrase\">to complete the cycle</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"water vapour condenses to form clouds","context":"Beginning at the evaporation stage, we can see that about 60% of water vapour in the air comes from the oceans. Heat from the sun causes water to evaporate, and water vapour condenses to form clouds. At the second stage, labelled ‘precipitation’ on the diagram, water falls as rain or snow."},{"en":"may take various paths","context":"At the third stage in the cycle, rainwater may take various paths. Some of it may fall into lakes or return to the oceans via ‘surface runoff’. Otherwise, rainwater may filter through the ground, reaching the impervious layer of the earth. Salt water intrusion is shown to take place just before groundwater passes into the oceans to complete the cycle."},{"en":"filter through the ground","context":"At the third stage in the cycle, rainwater may take various paths. Some of it may fall into lakes or return to the oceans via ‘surface runoff’. Otherwise, rainwater may filter through the ground, reaching the impervious layer of the earth. Salt water intrusion is shown to take place just before groundwater passes into the oceans to complete the cycle."},{"en":"the impervious layer of the earth","context":"At the third stage in the cycle, rainwater may take various paths. Some of it may fall into lakes or return to the oceans via ‘surface runoff’. Otherwise, rainwater may filter through the ground, reaching the impervious layer of the earth. Salt water intrusion is shown to take place just before groundwater passes into the oceans to complete the cycle."},{"en":"to complete the cycle","context":"At the third stage in the cycle, rainwater may take various paths. Some of it may fall into lakes or return to the oceans via ‘surface runoff’. Otherwise, rainwater may filter through the ground, reaching the impervious layer of the earth. Salt water intrusion is shown to take place just before groundwater passes into the oceans to complete the cycle."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-033-1"]},"_displayNo":"#038","summary":"Diagram below shows the water cycle, which is the continuous movement of…"},{"id":"writing-academic-038","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Processes & Cycles","title":"Hydroelectricity production","prompt":"The diagram below shows the process of using water to produce electricity.\nSummarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The picture explains the process through which water is used to produce electrical power.\n\nOverall, the process shows ten stages, using the movement of water to generate electricity for a range of different users. It begins with a natural cycle of evaporation and rainfall, and ends with power being distributed from a transformer station.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">The process begins when</strong> seawater is heated by the sun and evaporates to form small clouds in the sky. These clouds then <strong class=\"key-phrase\">merge into a storm cloud</strong>, which rains over the mountain, and the rainwater is gathered in the reservoir behind a dam.\n\nThe following steps involve a pipe which connects the reservoir to a turbine and a pump, and which is controlled using a valve. Once this valve is opened, water flows into the turbine and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rotates it to produce an electrical current</strong>, after which the water is pumped back into the reservoir.\n\nThe electricity produced by the turbine is then transferred to the transformer station through high voltage cables. At the last stage, the electrical power <strong class=\"key-phrase\">is delivered to domestic and industrial consumers</strong>, as well as to educational and medical facilities.","phrases":[{"en":"The process begins when","context":"The process begins when seawater is heated by the sun and evaporates to form small clouds in the sky. These clouds then merge into a storm cloud, which rains over the mountain, and the rainwater is gathered in the reservoir behind a dam."},{"en":"merge into a storm cloud","context":"The process begins when seawater is heated by the sun and evaporates to form small clouds in the sky. These clouds then merge into a storm cloud, which rains over the mountain, and the rainwater is gathered in the reservoir behind a dam."},{"en":"rotates it to produce an electrical current","context":"The following steps involve a pipe which connects the reservoir to a turbine and a pump, and which is controlled using a valve. Once this valve is opened, water flows into the turbine and rotates it to produce an electrical current, after which the water is pumped back into the reservoir."},{"en":"is delivered to domestic and industrial consumers","context":"The electricity produced by the turbine is then transferred to the transformer station through high voltage cables. At the last stage, the electrical power is delivered to domestic and industrial consumers, as well as to educational and medical facilities."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-038-1"]},"_displayNo":"#039","summary":"Diagram below shows the process of using water to produce electricity."},{"id":"writing-academic-040","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Processes & Cycles","title":"Urban waste sorting","prompt":"Write a description of how urban refuse is assorted for recycling. Use the information in the diagram and write a description of this process.","answer":"The diagram shows how urban refuse is collected, sorted and processed for recycling, and how the resulting products return to consumers.\n\nOverall, the system forms a loop. Recyclable materials pass from rubbish bins through recovery and manufacturing processes before reaching households, businesses and individual customers, who can then return suitable waste to the appropriate bins.\n\nAt the first stage, bottles made of plastic or glass, paper and durable items are collected from separate bins. They are taken to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">material recovery facilities</strong>, where the second stage involves sorting them into different streams. Soft drink containers are directed to soft drink companies, while newspapers and paper towels go to paper and pulp mills.\n\nThe remaining recyclables undergo further processing. They are cleaned, classified and crushed, after which businesses dealing in processed recyclables supply the materials to manufacturers. Engineering firms buy aluminium, whereas property developers purchase glass or plastic.\n\nThese different routes all lead to products that are supplied to households, businesses and individual customers. Once the products have been used, recyclable waste can be placed in the correct bins again, completing the cycle shown in the diagram.","phrases":[{"en":"material recovery facilities","context":"At the first stage, bottles made of plastic or glass, paper and durable items are collected from separate bins. They are taken to material recovery facilities, where the second stage involves sorting them into different streams. Soft drink containers are directed to soft drink companies, while newspapers and paper towels go to paper and pulp mills."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-040-1"]},"_displayNo":"#040","summary":"Write a description of how urban refuse is assorted for recycling."},{"id":"writing-academic-042","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Processes & Cycles","title":"Coffee production","prompt":"The diagram below shows how coffee is produced and prepared for sale in supermarkets and shops.","answer":"The picture illustrates the process of instant coffee manufacture and preparation for sale on the market.\n\nIt is clear that there are 11 stages in the production of instant coffee. The process begins with the picking of the beans and ends at the packing stage, and along the way the beans pass through heating, cooling, mixing, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">freezing and drying</strong>.\n\nLooking at the coffee production process in detail, coffee beans must first be picked in the fields. These beans are then dried, roasted and cooled, before being put into a grinding machine which <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turns them into ground coffee</strong>.\n\nAt the sixth stage in the process, the ground coffee is mixed with hot water, and the resulting mixture <strong class=\"key-phrase\">is strained</strong>. Next, the mixture is frozen and then passed once again through the grinder. After that, the ground, frozen mixture is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dried in a vacuum</strong> so that the water is removed, leaving the coffee granules that form the final product. Finally, these granules are packed into coffee jars ready for delivery to shops.","phrases":[{"en":"freezing and drying","context":"It is clear that there are 11 stages in the production of instant coffee. The process begins with the picking of the beans and ends at the packing stage, and along the way the beans pass through heating, cooling, mixing, freezing and drying."},{"en":"turns them into ground coffee","context":"Looking at the coffee production process in detail, coffee beans must first be picked in the fields. These beans are then dried, roasted and cooled, before being put into a grinding machine which turns them into ground coffee."},{"en":"is strained","context":"At the sixth stage in the process, the ground coffee is mixed with hot water, and the resulting mixture is strained. Next, the mixture is frozen and then passed once again through the grinder. After that, the ground, frozen mixture is dried in a vacuum so that the water is removed, leaving the coffee granules that form the final product. Finally, these granules are packed into coffee jars ready for delivery to shops."},{"en":"dried in a vacuum","context":"At the sixth stage in the process, the ground coffee is mixed with hot water, and the resulting mixture is strained. Next, the mixture is frozen and then passed once again through the grinder. After that, the ground, frozen mixture is dried in a vacuum so that the water is removed, leaving the coffee granules that form the final product. Finally, these granules are packed into coffee jars ready for delivery to shops."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-042-1"]},"_displayNo":"#041","summary":"Diagram below shows how coffee is produced and prepared for sale in…"},{"id":"writing-academic-043","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Processes & Cycles","title":"Weather forecasting process","prompt":"The diagram below shows how the Australian Bureau of Meteorology collects up-to-the-minute information on the weather in order to produce reliable forecasts","answer":"The figure illustrates the process used by the Australian Bureau of Meteorology to forecast the weather.\n\nThere are four stages in the process, beginning with the collection of information about the weather. This information is then analysed, prepared for presentation, and finally <strong class=\"key-phrase\">broadcast to the public</strong>.\n\nLooking at the first and second stages of the process, there are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">three ways of collecting weather data</strong> and three ways of analysing it. Firstly, incoming information can be received by satellite and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">presented for analysis as a satellite photo</strong>. Satellite information also contributes to the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">synoptic charts</strong> shown in the diagram. Secondly, incoming information may be collected directly by radar and analysed on a radar screen or synoptic chart. Finally, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">drifting buoys</strong> also collect data which can be shown on a synoptic chart.\n\nAt the third stage of the process, the weather broadcast is prepared on computers. Finally, it is delivered to the public on television, on the radio, or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">as a recorded telephone announcement</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"broadcast to the public","context":"There are four stages in the process, beginning with the collection of information about the weather. This information is then analysed, prepared for presentation, and finally broadcast to the public."},{"en":"three ways of collecting weather data","context":"Looking at the first and second stages of the process, there are three ways of collecting weather data and three ways of analysing it. Firstly, incoming information can be received by satellite and presented for analysis as a satellite photo. Satellite information also contributes to the synoptic charts shown in the diagram. Secondly, incoming information may be collected directly by radar and analysed on a radar screen or synoptic chart. Finally, drifting buoys also collect data which can be shown on a synoptic chart."},{"en":"presented for analysis as a satellite photo","context":"Looking at the first and second stages of the process, there are three ways of collecting weather data and three ways of analysing it. Firstly, incoming information can be received by satellite and presented for analysis as a satellite photo. Satellite information also contributes to the synoptic charts shown in the diagram. Secondly, incoming information may be collected directly by radar and analysed on a radar screen or synoptic chart. Finally, drifting buoys also collect data which can be shown on a synoptic chart."},{"en":"synoptic charts","context":"Looking at the first and second stages of the process, there are three ways of collecting weather data and three ways of analysing it. Firstly, incoming information can be received by satellite and presented for analysis as a satellite photo. Satellite information also contributes to the synoptic charts shown in the diagram. Secondly, incoming information may be collected directly by radar and analysed on a radar screen or synoptic chart. Finally, drifting buoys also collect data which can be shown on a synoptic chart."},{"en":"drifting buoys","context":"Looking at the first and second stages of the process, there are three ways of collecting weather data and three ways of analysing it. Firstly, incoming information can be received by satellite and presented for analysis as a satellite photo. Satellite information also contributes to the synoptic charts shown in the diagram. Secondly, incoming information may be collected directly by radar and analysed on a radar screen or synoptic chart. Finally, drifting buoys also collect data which can be shown on a synoptic chart."},{"en":"as a recorded telephone announcement","context":"At the third stage of the process, the weather broadcast is prepared on computers. Finally, it is delivered to the public on television, on the radio, or as a recorded telephone announcement."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-043-1"]},"_displayNo":"#042","summary":"Diagram below shows how the Australian Bureau of Meteorology collects…"},{"id":"writing-academic-044","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Processes & Cycles","title":"Salmon life cycle","prompt":"The diagram below shows the life cycle of a salmon, from egg to adult fish.","answer":"The diagram illustrates the stages in the life of the salmon, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">from birth to maturity</strong>.\n\nIt is clear that there are six main stages as the salmon develops from egg to mature adult. We can also see that salmon spend time in <strong class=\"key-phrase\">three distinct locations during the cycle</strong>, moving from river to estuary to ocean and then back upstream.\n\nSalmon begin their lives in rivers where the adult fish lay their eggs. Following incubation, the young fish emerge. They spend the next stage of their lives <strong class=\"key-phrase\">being reared in freshwater areas</strong>. Then, at some point in their development, the fish swim downstream to river estuaries where rearing continues.\n\nFollowing the estuary rearing period, the maturing salmon migrate to the ocean, where they eventually become fully grown adults. Finally, the adult fish travel back upstream to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spawning areas</strong> of rivers; here they reproduce and lay their eggs, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the life cycle begins anew</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"from birth to maturity","context":"The diagram illustrates the stages in the life of the salmon, from birth to maturity."},{"en":"three distinct locations during the cycle","context":"It is clear that there are six main stages as the salmon develops from egg to mature adult. We can also see that salmon spend time in three distinct locations during the cycle, moving from river to estuary to ocean and then back upstream."},{"en":"being reared in freshwater areas","context":"Salmon begin their lives in rivers where the adult fish lay their eggs. Following incubation, the young fish emerge. They spend the next stage of their lives being reared in freshwater areas. Then, at some point in their development, the fish swim downstream to river estuaries where rearing continues."},{"en":"spawning areas","context":"Following the estuary rearing period, the maturing salmon migrate to the ocean, where they eventually become fully grown adults. Finally, the adult fish travel back upstream to spawning areas of rivers; here they reproduce and lay their eggs, and the life cycle begins anew."},{"en":"the life cycle begins anew","context":"Following the estuary rearing period, the maturing salmon migrate to the ocean, where they eventually become fully grown adults. Finally, the adult fish travel back upstream to spawning areas of rivers; here they reproduce and lay their eggs, and the life cycle begins anew."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-044-1"]},"_displayNo":"#043","summary":"Diagram below shows the life cycle of a salmon, from egg to adult fish."},{"id":"writing-academic-046","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Processes & Cycles","title":"Silkworm and silk production","prompt":"The diagrams below show the life cycle of the silkworm and the stages in the production of silk","answer":"The diagrams illustrate the stages in the life of a silkworm and the process of producing silk cloth.\n\nThere are four main stages in the life cycle of the silkworm, from eggs to adult moth. The process of silk cloth production involves six steps, from silkworm cocoon to silk material.\n\nAt the first stage in the life cycle of a silkworm, the moth lays its eggs. Around ten days later, silkworm <strong class=\"key-phrase\">larvae hatch from the eggs</strong>; these larvae <strong class=\"key-phrase\">feed on mulberry leaves</strong>. Then, after four to six weeks, the larvae <strong class=\"key-phrase\">become covered in</strong> silk thread, and it takes between three and eight days for a full cocoon to be produced. After a further sixteen days, an adult moth emerges, completing the life cycle.\n\nFor silk cloth production, a cocoon is first selected and then boiled in water. After boiling, the silk thread that makes up the cocoon <strong class=\"key-phrase\">is unwound</strong>, and then several strands of thread <strong class=\"key-phrase\">are twisted together</strong>. At the fifth and sixth stages in the process, the thread can either be dyed and then <strong class=\"key-phrase\">woven into silk fabric</strong>, or it can be woven first and then dyed subsequently.","phrases":[{"en":"larvae hatch from the eggs","context":"At the first stage in the life cycle of a silkworm, the moth lays its eggs. Around ten days later, silkworm larvae hatch from the eggs; these larvae feed on mulberry leaves. Then, after four to six weeks, the larvae become covered in silk thread, and it takes between three and eight days for a full cocoon to be produced. After a further sixteen days, an adult moth emerges, completing the life cycle."},{"en":"feed on mulberry leaves","context":"At the first stage in the life cycle of a silkworm, the moth lays its eggs. Around ten days later, silkworm larvae hatch from the eggs; these larvae feed on mulberry leaves. Then, after four to six weeks, the larvae become covered in silk thread, and it takes between three and eight days for a full cocoon to be produced. After a further sixteen days, an adult moth emerges, completing the life cycle."},{"en":"become covered in","context":"At the first stage in the life cycle of a silkworm, the moth lays its eggs. Around ten days later, silkworm larvae hatch from the eggs; these larvae feed on mulberry leaves. Then, after four to six weeks, the larvae become covered in silk thread, and it takes between three and eight days for a full cocoon to be produced. After a further sixteen days, an adult moth emerges, completing the life cycle."},{"en":"is unwound","context":"For silk cloth production, a cocoon is first selected and then boiled in water. After boiling, the silk thread that makes up the cocoon is unwound, and then several strands of thread are twisted together. At the fifth and sixth stages in the process, the thread can either be dyed and then woven into silk fabric, or it can be woven first and then dyed subsequently."},{"en":"are twisted together","context":"For silk cloth production, a cocoon is first selected and then boiled in water. After boiling, the silk thread that makes up the cocoon is unwound, and then several strands of thread are twisted together. At the fifth and sixth stages in the process, the thread can either be dyed and then woven into silk fabric, or it can be woven first and then dyed subsequently."},{"en":"woven into silk fabric","context":"For silk cloth production, a cocoon is first selected and then boiled in water. After boiling, the silk thread that makes up the cocoon is unwound, and then several strands of thread are twisted together. At the fifth and sixth stages in the process, the thread can either be dyed and then woven into silk fabric, or it can be woven first and then dyed subsequently."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-046-1"]},"_displayNo":"#044","summary":"Diagrams below show the life cycle of the silkworm and the stages in the…"},{"id":"writing-academic-056","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Processes & Cycles","title":"Honey bee life cycle","prompt":"The diagram below shows the life cycle of the honey bee.\nSummarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The diagram illustrates the stages in the life of honey bees. Overall, the cycle shown comprises six stages from egg to full maturity, and takes approximately five weeks (34 to 36 days to be exact) to complete.\n\nThe first stage is when the female bee lays up to a couple of oval eggs once every 72 hours. These eggs hatch between nine and ten days later, and the immature forms labelled nymphs in the diagram emerge.\n\nOver the next 3 weeks the nymphs go through three moulting stages, shedding their skins to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">allow further growth to occur</strong>: the first moulting happens 5 days after the eggs hatch, and the second one takes place a week later. Nine days after that, the nymphs moult for a third time and young adults emerge. These take four more days to reach full maturity, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the cycle starts over again</strong>. Of the individual intervals given, the nine-to-ten-day period before hatching is the longest, while the final maturation stage lasts only four days.","phrases":[{"en":"allow further growth to occur","context":"Over the next 3 weeks the nymphs go through three moulting stages, shedding their skins to allow further growth to occur: the first moulting happens 5 days after the eggs hatch, and the second one takes place a week later. Nine days after that, the nymphs moult for a third time and young adults emerge. These take four more days to reach full maturity, and the cycle starts over again. Of the individual intervals given, the nine-to-ten-day period before hatching is the longest, while the final maturation stage lasts only four days."},{"en":"the cycle starts over again","context":"Over the next 3 weeks the nymphs go through three moulting stages, shedding their skins to allow further growth to occur: the first moulting happens 5 days after the eggs hatch, and the second one takes place a week later. Nine days after that, the nymphs moult for a third time and young adults emerge. These take four more days to reach full maturity, and the cycle starts over again. Of the individual intervals given, the nine-to-ten-day period before hatching is the longest, while the final maturation stage lasts only four days."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-056-1"]},"_displayNo":"#045","summary":"Diagram below shows the life cycle of the honey bee."},{"id":"writing-academic-008","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Sport & Leisure","title":"Teen internet and gaming time","prompt":"The chart shows the amount of time that 10- to 15-year-olds in the UK spend chatting on the Internet and playing games on consoles on an average school day.","answer":"The bar chart compares the time spent by 10 to 15-year-olds in the UK on two activities, namely chatting online and playing computer games.\n\nOverall, we can see that playing computer games is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">marginally more popular than</strong> chatting on the Internet. However, completely different trends can be seen if we look at the specific figures for boys and girls.\n\nBoys aged between 10 and 15 clearly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">favour</strong> playing on games consoles over chatting online. According to the chart, while 85% of boys play computer games every day, only 55% chat online daily. The majority of boys also play on their consoles for more than one hour each day, and 10% do so for four hours or more.\n\nBy contrast, girls prefer chatting online. Close to 70% of 10 to 15-year-old girls <strong class=\"key-phrase\">engage in online conversation each day</strong>, compared to about 50% of this cohort who play computer games. Of the girls who do play on consoles, most of them play <strong class=\"key-phrase\">for less than an hour</strong>, whereas most girls who chat online do so for more than one hour, and nearly 10% chat for four hours or more.","phrases":[{"en":"marginally more popular than","context":"Overall, we can see that playing computer games is marginally more popular than chatting on the Internet. However, completely different trends can be seen if we look at the specific figures for boys and girls."},{"en":"favour","context":"Boys aged between 10 and 15 clearly favour playing on games consoles over chatting online. According to the chart, while 85% of boys play computer games every day, only 55% chat online daily. The majority of boys also play on their consoles for more than one hour each day, and 10% do so for four hours or more."},{"en":"engage in online conversation each day","context":"By contrast, girls prefer chatting online. Close to 70% of 10 to 15-year-old girls engage in online conversation each day, compared to about 50% of this cohort who play computer games. Of the girls who do play on consoles, most of them play for less than an hour, whereas most girls who chat online do so for more than one hour, and nearly 10% chat for four hours or more."},{"en":"for less than an hour","context":"By contrast, girls prefer chatting online. Close to 70% of 10 to 15-year-old girls engage in online conversation each day, compared to about 50% of this cohort who play computer games. Of the girls who do play on consoles, most of them play for less than an hour, whereas most girls who chat online do so for more than one hour, and nearly 10% chat for four hours or more."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-008-1"]},"_displayNo":"#046","summary":"Chart below shows the amount of"},{"id":"writing-academic-011","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Sport & Leisure","title":"Olympic medal totals","prompt":"The chart below shows the total number of Olympic medals won by twelve different countries.","answer":"The bar chart compares twelve countries in terms of the overall medal totals recorded for them <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in the chart</strong> at the Olympic Games.\n\nIt is clear that the USA was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">by far the most successful</strong> Olympic medal-winning nation in the chart. It is also noticeable that the figures for gold, silver and bronze medals won by any particular country <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tend to be fairly similar</strong>.\n\nThe USA had won a total of around 2,300 Olympic medals, including approximately 900 gold medals, 750 silver and 650 bronze. In second place in the chart was the Soviet Union, with just over 1,000 medals. Again, the number of gold medals won by this country is slightly higher than the number of silver or bronze medals.\n\nOnly four other countries - the UK, France, Germany and Italy - had won more than 500 Olympic medals, all with similar proportions of each medal colour. Apart from the USA and the Soviet Union, China was the only other country with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a noticeably higher proportion of gold medals</strong> (about 200) compared to silver and bronze (about 100 each).","phrases":[{"en":"in the chart","context":"The bar chart compares twelve countries in terms of the overall medal totals recorded for them in the chart at the Olympic Games."},{"en":"by far the most successful","context":"It is clear that the USA was by far the most successful Olympic medal-winning nation in the chart. It is also noticeable that the figures for gold, silver and bronze medals won by any particular country tend to be fairly similar."},{"en":"tend to be fairly similar","context":"It is clear that the USA was by far the most successful Olympic medal-winning nation in the chart. It is also noticeable that the figures for gold, silver and bronze medals won by any particular country tend to be fairly similar."},{"en":"a noticeably higher proportion of gold medals","context":"Only four other countries - the UK, France, Germany and Italy - had won more than 500 Olympic medals, all with similar proportions of each medal colour. Apart from the USA and the Soviet Union, China was the only other country with a noticeably higher proportion of gold medals (about 200) compared to silver and bronze (about 100 each)."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-011-1"]},"_displayNo":"#047","summary":"Chart below shows the total number of Olympic medals won by twelve…"},{"id":"writing-academic-016","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Sport & Leisure","title":"Children's sports spending","prompt":"The first chart shows British parents' spending on children's sports from 2008 to 2014, while the second shows the number of children participating in three sports.","answer":"The line graphs show the average monthly amount that parents in Britain spent on their children&#x27;s sporting activities and the number of British children who took part in three different sports from 2008 to 2014.\n\nIt is clear that parents spent more money each year on their children&#x27;s participation in sports over the six-year period. In terms of the number of children taking part, football was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">significantly more popular than athletics and swimming</strong>.\n\nIn 2008, British parents spent an average of around £20 per month on their children&#x27;s sporting activities. Parents’ spending on children&#x27;s sports <strong class=\"key-phrase\">increased gradually over the following six years</strong>, and by 2014 the average monthly amount had risen to just over £30.\n\nLooking at participation numbers, in 2008 approximately 8 million British children played football, while only 2 million children <strong class=\"key-phrase\">were enrolled in swimming clubs</strong> and less than 1 million practised athletics. The figures for football participation remained relatively stable over the following 6 years. By contrast, participation in swimming <strong class=\"key-phrase\">almost doubled, to nearly 4 million children</strong>, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">there was a near fivefold increase in</strong> the number of children doing athletics.","phrases":[{"en":"significantly more popular than athletics and swimming","context":"It is clear that parents spent more money each year on their children's participation in sports over the six-year period. In terms of the number of children taking part, football was significantly more popular than athletics and swimming."},{"en":"increased gradually over the following six years","context":"In 2008, British parents spent an average of around £20 per month on their children's sporting activities. Parents’ spending on children's sports increased gradually over the following six years, and by 2014 the average monthly amount had risen to just over £30."},{"en":"were enrolled in swimming clubs","context":"Looking at participation numbers, in 2008 approximately 8 million British children played football, while only 2 million children were enrolled in swimming clubs and less than 1 million practised athletics. The figures for football participation remained relatively stable over the following 6 years. By contrast, participation in swimming almost doubled, to nearly 4 million children, and there was a near fivefold increase in the number of children doing athletics."},{"en":"almost doubled, to nearly 4 million children","context":"Looking at participation numbers, in 2008 approximately 8 million British children played football, while only 2 million children were enrolled in swimming clubs and less than 1 million practised athletics. The figures for football participation remained relatively stable over the following 6 years. By contrast, participation in swimming almost doubled, to nearly 4 million children, and there was a near fivefold increase in the number of children doing athletics."},{"en":"there was a near fivefold increase in","context":"Looking at participation numbers, in 2008 approximately 8 million British children played football, while only 2 million children were enrolled in swimming clubs and less than 1 million practised athletics. The figures for football participation remained relatively stable over the following 6 years. By contrast, participation in swimming almost doubled, to nearly 4 million children, and there was a near fivefold increase in the number of children doing athletics."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-016-1"]},"_displayNo":"#048","summary":"First chart below gives information about the money spent by British…"},{"id":"writing-academic-010","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Technology & Communication","title":"UK telephone calls","prompt":"The chart shows the total number of minutes, in billions, spent on three types of telephone calls in the UK between 1995 and 2002.","answer":"The bar chart compares the amount of time spent by people in the UK on three different types of phone call between 1995 and 2002.\n\nIt is clear that calls made via local, fixed lines were the most popular type, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in terms of overall usage</strong>, throughout the period shown. The lowest figures on the chart are for mobile calls, but this category also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">saw the most dramatic increase in</strong> user minutes.\n\nIn 1995, people in the UK used fixed lines for a total of just over 70 billion minutes for local calls, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">about half of that amount of time</strong> for national or international calls. By contrast, mobile phones were only used for around 4 billion minutes. Over the following four years, the figures for all three types of phone call increased steadily.\n\nBy 1999, the amount of time spent on local calls using landlines had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reached a peak</strong> at 90 billion minutes. Subsequently, the figure for this category fell, but the rise in the other two types of phone call continued. In 2002, the number of minutes of national / international landline calls passed 60 billion, while the figure for mobiles rose to around 45 billion minutes.","phrases":[{"en":"in terms of overall usage","context":"It is clear that calls made via local, fixed lines were the most popular type, in terms of overall usage, throughout the period shown. The lowest figures on the chart are for mobile calls, but this category also saw the most dramatic increase in user minutes."},{"en":"saw the most dramatic increase in","context":"It is clear that calls made via local, fixed lines were the most popular type, in terms of overall usage, throughout the period shown. The lowest figures on the chart are for mobile calls, but this category also saw the most dramatic increase in user minutes."},{"en":"about half of that amount of time","context":"In 1995, people in the UK used fixed lines for a total of just over 70 billion minutes for local calls, and about half of that amount of time for national or international calls. By contrast, mobile phones were only used for around 4 billion minutes. Over the following four years, the figures for all three types of phone call increased steadily."},{"en":"reached a peak","context":"By 1999, the amount of time spent on local calls using landlines had reached a peak at 90 billion minutes. Subsequently, the figure for this category fell, but the rise in the other two types of phone call continued. In 2002, the number of minutes of national / international landline calls passed 60 billion, while the figure for mobiles rose to around 45 billion minutes."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-010-1"]},"_displayNo":"#049","summary":"Chart below shows the total number of minutes (in billions) of telephone…"},{"id":"writing-academic-041","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Technology & Communication","title":"Digital game sales","prompt":"The bar graph shows the global sales (in billions of dollars) of different types of digital games","answer":"The bar chart compares the turnover in dollars from sales of video games for four different platforms, namely mobile phones, online, consoles and handheld devices, from 2000 to 2006.\n\nIt is clear that sales of games for three out of the four platforms rose each year, leading to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a significant rise</strong> in total global turnover over the 7-year period. Sales figures for handheld games were <strong class=\"key-phrase\">at least twice as high as</strong> those for any other platform in almost every year.\n\nIn 2000, worldwide sales of handheld games stood at around $11 billion, while console games earned just under $6 billion. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">No figures are given for</strong> mobile or online games in that year. Over the next 3 years, sales of handheld video games rose by about $4 billion, but the figure for consoles decreased by $2 billion. Mobile phone and online games started to become popular, with sales reaching around $3 billion in 2003.\n\nIn 2006, sales of handheld, online and mobile games <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reached peaks</strong> of 17, 9 and 7 billion dollars respectively. By contrast, turnover from console games <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dropped to its lowest point</strong>, at around $2.5 billion.","phrases":[{"en":"a significant rise","context":"It is clear that sales of games for three out of the four platforms rose each year, leading to a significant rise in total global turnover over the 7-year period. Sales figures for handheld games were at least twice as high as those for any other platform in almost every year."},{"en":"at least twice as high as","context":"It is clear that sales of games for three out of the four platforms rose each year, leading to a significant rise in total global turnover over the 7-year period. Sales figures for handheld games were at least twice as high as those for any other platform in almost every year."},{"en":"No figures are given for","context":"In 2000, worldwide sales of handheld games stood at around $11 billion, while console games earned just under $6 billion. No figures are given for mobile or online games in that year. Over the next 3 years, sales of handheld video games rose by about $4 billion, but the figure for consoles decreased by $2 billion. Mobile phone and online games started to become popular, with sales reaching around $3 billion in 2003."},{"en":"reached peaks","context":"In 2006, sales of handheld, online and mobile games reached peaks of 17, 9 and 7 billion dollars respectively. By contrast, turnover from console games dropped to its lowest point, at around $2.5 billion."},{"en":"dropped to its lowest point","context":"In 2006, sales of handheld, online and mobile games reached peaks of 17, 9 and 7 billion dollars respectively. By contrast, turnover from console games dropped to its lowest point, at around $2.5 billion."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-041-1"]},"_displayNo":"#050","summary":"Bar graph shows the global sales (in billions of dollars) of different…"},{"id":"writing-academic-049","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Technology & Communication","title":"US phone expenditure","prompt":"The graph compares US consumers' average annual expenditure on cell-phone and residential phone services between 2001 and 2010.","answer":"The line graph compares the average amount of money spent each year by consumers in the United States on mobile phone services and on residential landline services between 2001 and 2010.\n\nIt is clear that spending on landline phones <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fell steadily</strong> over the 10-year period, while mobile phone expenditure rose quickly. The year 2006 <strong class=\"key-phrase\">marks the point at which</strong> the two types of expenditure were approximately equal; mobile services became the higher figure afterwards.\n\nIn 2001, US consumers spent an average of nearly $700 on residential phone services, compared to only around $200 on cell phone services. Over the following five years, average yearly spending on landlines <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dropped by nearly $200</strong>. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">By contrast</strong>, expenditure on mobiles rose by approximately $300.\n\nIn the year 2006, the average American paid out the same amount of money on both types of phone service, spending just over $500 on each. By 2010, expenditure on mobile phones <strong class=\"key-phrase\">had reached around $750</strong>, while the figure for spending on residential services had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fallen to just over half this amount</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"fell steadily","context":"It is clear that spending on landline phones fell steadily over the 10-year period, while mobile phone expenditure rose quickly. The year 2006 marks the point at which the two types of expenditure were approximately equal; mobile services became the higher figure afterwards."},{"en":"marks the point at which","context":"It is clear that spending on landline phones fell steadily over the 10-year period, while mobile phone expenditure rose quickly. The year 2006 marks the point at which the two types of expenditure were approximately equal; mobile services became the higher figure afterwards."},{"en":"dropped by nearly $200","context":"In 2001, US consumers spent an average of nearly $700 on residential phone services, compared to only around $200 on cell phone services. Over the following five years, average yearly spending on landlines dropped by nearly $200. By contrast, expenditure on mobiles rose by approximately $300."},{"en":"By contrast","context":"In 2001, US consumers spent an average of nearly $700 on residential phone services, compared to only around $200 on cell phone services. Over the following five years, average yearly spending on landlines dropped by nearly $200. By contrast, expenditure on mobiles rose by approximately $300."},{"en":"had reached around $750","context":"In the year 2006, the average American paid out the same amount of money on both types of phone service, spending just over $500 on each. By 2010, expenditure on mobile phones had reached around $750, while the figure for spending on residential services had fallen to just over half this amount."},{"en":"fallen to just over half this amount","context":"In the year 2006, the average American paid out the same amount of money on both types of phone service, spending just over $500 on each. By 2010, expenditure on mobile phones had reached around $750, while the figure for spending on residential services had fallen to just over half this amount."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-049-1"]},"_displayNo":"#051","summary":"Graph below shows US consumers' average annual expenditures on cell phone…"},{"id":"writing-academic-054","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Technology & Communication","title":"Mobile phone feature use","prompt":"The table below shows the percentage of mobile phones owners using various mobile features.","answer":"The table compares the percentages of people using different functions of their mobile phones between 2006 and 2010.\n\nThroughout the period shown, making calls was the most widely used mobile phone function. However, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">there was a marked increase in</strong> the popularity of other mobile phone features, particularly the Internet search feature.\n\nIn 2006, 100% of mobile phone owners used their phones to make calls, while <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the next most popular functions were</strong> text messaging (73%) and taking photos (66%). <strong class=\"key-phrase\">By contrast</strong>, less than 20% of owners played games or music on their phones, and there were no figures for users doing Internet searches or recording video.\n\nOver the following 4 years, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">there was relatively little change in</strong> the figures for the top three mobile phone features. However, the percentage of people using their phones to access the Internet <strong class=\"key-phrase\">jumped to 41% in 2008</strong> and then to 73% in 2010. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">There was also a significant rise in</strong> the use of mobiles to play games and to record video, with figures reaching 41% and 35% <strong class=\"key-phrase\">respectively</strong> in 2010.","phrases":[{"en":"there was a marked increase in","context":"Throughout the period shown, making calls was the most widely used mobile phone function. However, there was a marked increase in the popularity of other mobile phone features, particularly the Internet search feature."},{"en":"the next most popular functions were","context":"In 2006, 100% of mobile phone owners used their phones to make calls, while the next most popular functions were text messaging (73%) and taking photos (66%). By contrast, less than 20% of owners played games or music on their phones, and there were no figures for users doing Internet searches or recording video."},{"en":"By contrast","context":"In 2006, 100% of mobile phone owners used their phones to make calls, while the next most popular functions were text messaging (73%) and taking photos (66%). By contrast, less than 20% of owners played games or music on their phones, and there were no figures for users doing Internet searches or recording video."},{"en":"there was relatively little change in","context":"Over the following 4 years, there was relatively little change in the figures for the top three mobile phone features. However, the percentage of people using their phones to access the Internet jumped to 41% in 2008 and then to 73% in 2010. There was also a significant rise in the use of mobiles to play games and to record video, with figures reaching 41% and 35% respectively in 2010."},{"en":"jumped to 41% in 2008","context":"Over the following 4 years, there was relatively little change in the figures for the top three mobile phone features. However, the percentage of people using their phones to access the Internet jumped to 41% in 2008 and then to 73% in 2010. There was also a significant rise in the use of mobiles to play games and to record video, with figures reaching 41% and 35% respectively in 2010."},{"en":"There was also a significant rise in","context":"Over the following 4 years, there was relatively little change in the figures for the top three mobile phone features. However, the percentage of people using their phones to access the Internet jumped to 41% in 2008 and then to 73% in 2010. There was also a significant rise in the use of mobiles to play games and to record video, with figures reaching 41% and 35% respectively in 2010."},{"en":"respectively","context":"Over the following 4 years, there was relatively little change in the figures for the top three mobile phone features. However, the percentage of people using their phones to access the Internet jumped to 41% in 2008 and then to 73% in 2010. There was also a significant rise in the use of mobiles to play games and to record video, with figures reaching 41% and 35% respectively in 2010."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-054-1"]},"_displayNo":"#052","summary":"Table below shows the percentage of mobile phones owners using various…"},{"id":"writing-academic-009","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Tourism & Travel","title":"British tourist attractions","prompt":"The chart below shows the results of a survey of people who visited four types of tourist attraction in Britain in the year 1999.","answer":"The pie chart compares figures for visitors to four categories of tourist attraction and to five different theme parks in Britain in 1999.\n\nIt is clear that theme parks and museums/ galleries were the two most popular types of tourist attraction in that year. Blackpool Pleasure Beach received by far the highest proportion of visitors in the theme park sector.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">Looking at the information in more detail</strong>, we can see that 38% of the surveyed visitors went to a theme park, and 37% of them went to a museum or gallery. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">By contrast</strong>, historic houses and monuments were visited by only 16% of the sample, while wildlife parks and zoos were the least popular of the four types of tourist attraction, with only 9% of visitors.\n\nIn the theme park sector, almost half of theme park visitors (47%) had been to Blackpool Pleasure Beach. Alton Towers was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the second most popular amusement park</strong>, with 17% of theme park visitors, followed by Pleasureland in Southport, with 16%. Finally, Chessington World of Adventures and Legoland Windsor had each welcomed 10% of theme park visitors.","phrases":[{"en":"Looking at the information in more detail","context":"Looking at the information in more detail, we can see that 38% of the surveyed visitors went to a theme park, and 37% of them went to a museum or gallery. By contrast, historic houses and monuments were visited by only 16% of the sample, while wildlife parks and zoos were the least popular of the four types of tourist attraction, with only 9% of visitors."},{"en":"By contrast","context":"Looking at the information in more detail, we can see that 38% of the surveyed visitors went to a theme park, and 37% of them went to a museum or gallery. By contrast, historic houses and monuments were visited by only 16% of the sample, while wildlife parks and zoos were the least popular of the four types of tourist attraction, with only 9% of visitors."},{"en":"the second most popular amusement park","context":"In the theme park sector, almost half of theme park visitors (47%) had been to Blackpool Pleasure Beach. Alton Towers was the second most popular amusement park, with 17% of theme park visitors, followed by Pleasureland in Southport, with 16%. Finally, Chessington World of Adventures and Legoland Windsor had each welcomed 10% of theme park visitors."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-009-1"]},"_displayNo":"#053","summary":"Chart below shows the results of a survey of people who visited four types…"},{"id":"writing-academic-014","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Tourism & Travel","title":"US travel patterns and problems","prompt":"The charts below show reasons for travel and the main issues for the travelling public in the US in 2009.","answer":"The bar chart and pie chart give information about why US residents travelled and what travel problems they experienced in the year 2009.\n\nIt is clear that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the principal reason why Americans travelled</strong> in 2009 was to commute to and from work. In the same year, the primary concern of Americans, with regard to the trips they made, was the cost of travelling.\n\nLooking more closely at the bar chart, we can see that 49% of the trips made by Americans in 2009 were for the purpose of commuting. By contrast, only 6% of trips were visits to friends or relatives, and one in ten trips were for social or recreation reasons. Shopping <strong class=\"key-phrase\">was cited as</strong> the reason for 16% of all travel, while unspecified ‘personal reasons’ <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accounted for the remaining</strong> 19%.\n\nAccording to the pie chart, price was the key consideration for 36% of American travellers. Almost one in five people cited safety as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">their foremost travel concern</strong>, while aggressive driving and highway congestion were the main issues for 17% and 14% of the travelling public. Finally, a total of 14% of those surveyed thought that access to public transport or space for pedestrians were the most important travel issues.","phrases":[{"en":"the principal reason why Americans travelled","context":"It is clear that the principal reason why Americans travelled in 2009 was to commute to and from work. In the same year, the primary concern of Americans, with regard to the trips they made, was the cost of travelling."},{"en":"was cited as","context":"Looking more closely at the bar chart, we can see that 49% of the trips made by Americans in 2009 were for the purpose of commuting. By contrast, only 6% of trips were visits to friends or relatives, and one in ten trips were for social or recreation reasons. Shopping was cited as the reason for 16% of all travel, while unspecified ‘personal reasons’ accounted for the remaining 19%."},{"en":"accounted for the remaining","context":"Looking more closely at the bar chart, we can see that 49% of the trips made by Americans in 2009 were for the purpose of commuting. By contrast, only 6% of trips were visits to friends or relatives, and one in ten trips were for social or recreation reasons. Shopping was cited as the reason for 16% of all travel, while unspecified ‘personal reasons’ accounted for the remaining 19%."},{"en":"their foremost travel concern","context":"According to the pie chart, price was the key consideration for 36% of American travellers. Almost one in five people cited safety as their foremost travel concern, while aggressive driving and highway congestion were the main issues for 17% and 14% of the travelling public. Finally, a total of 14% of those surveyed thought that access to public transport or space for pedestrians were the most important travel issues."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-014-1"]},"_displayNo":"#054","summary":"Charts below show reasons for travel and the main issues for the travelling…"},{"id":"writing-academic-015","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Tourism & Travel","title":"Parkway Hotel customer service","prompt":"The charts show how visitors rated the Parkway Hotel's customer service in 2005 and 2010.","answer":"The pie charts compare visitors’ responses to a survey about customer service at the Parkway Hotel in 2005 and in 2010.\n\nIt is clear that overall customer satisfaction increased considerably from 2005 to 2010. While most hotel guests rated customer service as satisfactory or poor in 2005, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a clear majority</strong> described the hotel&#x27;s service as good or excellent in 2010.\n\nLooking at the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">positive responses</strong> first, in 2005 only 5% of the hotel&#x27;s visitors rated its customer service as excellent, but this figure rose to 28% in 2010. There was also a rise in the number of ‘good’ ratings: only 14% of guests described customer service in the hotel as good in 2005, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">almost three times as many</strong> people gave this rating five years later.\n\nWith regard to negative feedback, the proportion of guests who considered the hotel&#x27;s customer service to be poor fell from 21% in 2005 to only 12% in 2010. Similarly, the proportion of people who thought customer service was very poor <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dropped from 15% to only 4%</strong> over the 5-year period. Finally, a fall in the number of ‘satisfactory’ ratings in 2010 reflects the fact that more people gave positive responses to the survey in that year.","phrases":[{"en":"a clear majority","context":"It is clear that overall customer satisfaction increased considerably from 2005 to 2010. While most hotel guests rated customer service as satisfactory or poor in 2005, a clear majority described the hotel's service as good or excellent in 2010."},{"en":"positive responses","context":"Looking at the positive responses first, in 2005 only 5% of the hotel's visitors rated its customer service as excellent, but this figure rose to 28% in 2010. There was also a rise in the number of ‘good’ ratings: only 14% of guests described customer service in the hotel as good in 2005, but almost three times as many people gave this rating five years later."},{"en":"almost three times as many","context":"Looking at the positive responses first, in 2005 only 5% of the hotel's visitors rated its customer service as excellent, but this figure rose to 28% in 2010. There was also a rise in the number of ‘good’ ratings: only 14% of guests described customer service in the hotel as good in 2005, but almost three times as many people gave this rating five years later."},{"en":"dropped from 15% to only 4%","context":"With regard to negative feedback, the proportion of guests who considered the hotel's customer service to be poor fell from 21% in 2005 to only 12% in 2010. Similarly, the proportion of people who thought customer service was very poor dropped from 15% to only 4% over the 5-year period. Finally, a fall in the number of ‘satisfactory’ ratings in 2010 reflects the fact that more people gave positive responses to the survey in that year."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-015-1"]},"_displayNo":"#055","summary":"Charts below show the results of a questionnaire that asked visitors to the…"},{"id":"writing-academic-020","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Tourism & Travel","title":"UK travel and destinations","prompt":"The charts below give information about travel to and from the UK, and about the most popular countries for UK residents to visit.\nSummarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The line graph compares visits abroad by UK residents with visits to the UK by overseas residents between 1979 and 1999. The bar chart shows the five most popular overseas destinations for UK residents in 1999.\n\nOverall, both types of visit increased, but outbound travel grew more strongly and remained higher throughout <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the period shown</strong>. France and Spain were the leading destinations among the five countries listed.\n\nIn 1979, UK residents made approximately 12 million visits abroad, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">compared with</strong> about 10 million visits to the UK by overseas residents. Outbound visits then rose substantially, reaching roughly 53 million in 1999, more than four times the initial figure. Inbound visits increased more moderately to approximately 27 million, or around half the outbound total at the end of the period.\n\nFrance received about 11 million visits from UK residents in 1999, followed by Spain with roughly 9 to 10 million. The other three destinations were considerably less popular: the USA, Greece and Turkey each recorded between approximately 2 and 4 million visits. Turkey had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the lowest figure</strong>, at around 2 million, less than one-fifth of the French total. The charts count visits, so the figures do not necessarily represent different individual travellers.","phrases":[{"en":"the period shown","context":"Overall, both types of visit increased, but outbound travel grew more strongly and remained higher throughout the period shown. France and Spain were the leading destinations among the five countries listed."},{"en":"compared with","context":"In 1979, UK residents made approximately 12 million visits abroad, compared with about 10 million visits to the UK by overseas residents. Outbound visits then rose substantially, reaching roughly 53 million in 1999, more than four times the initial figure. Inbound visits increased more moderately to approximately 27 million, or around half the outbound total at the end of the period."},{"en":"the lowest figure","context":"France received about 11 million visits from UK residents in 1999, followed by Spain with roughly 9 to 10 million. The other three destinations were considerably less popular: the USA, Greece and Turkey each recorded between approximately 2 and 4 million visits. Turkey had the lowest figure, at around 2 million, less than one-fifth of the French total. The charts count visits, so the figures do not necessarily represent different individual travellers."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-020-1"]},"_displayNo":"#056","summary":"Charts below give information about travel to and from the UK, and about…"},{"id":"writing-academic-017","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Trade & Industry","title":"Oil use and employment","prompt":"Chart A below shows the rate of oil consumption (in millions of barrels) in a small country from 1950 to 2010. Chart B shows the number of people employed in the oil industry in that country, and also the rate of unemployment throughout the country over the same time. Write a report summarising the information. Select and describe the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The two charts show the rate of oil consumption, the number of people employed in the oil industry, and the rate of unemployment in a small country between 1950 and 2010.\n\nOverall, the rise in oil use in this country was matched by a roughly similar <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rate of growth</strong> in the number of people employed in the oil business. At the same time, unemployment in the country as a whole dropped by more than half.\n\nOil consumption fluctuated at around 20 million barrels per year until 1970, but it then rose steadily, reaching 40 million barrels in 2000 and around 50 million by 2010.\n\nTurning to the second chart, the number of people employed in the oil industry <strong class=\"key-phrase\">roughly tripled over the same period</strong>. This figure also fluctuated at around 1,000 until 1970, before climbing consistently, passing 1,800 in 1980 and reaching 3,100 in 2010. National unemployment, on the other hand, rose slightly from 4% in 1950 to 4.1% in 1960, before falling steadily to 3.1% in 1980 and 1.6% in 2010.","phrases":[{"en":"rate of growth","context":"Overall, the rise in oil use in this country was matched by a roughly similar rate of growth in the number of people employed in the oil business. At the same time, unemployment in the country as a whole dropped by more than half."},{"en":"roughly tripled over the same period","context":"Turning to the second chart, the number of people employed in the oil industry roughly tripled over the same period. This figure also fluctuated at around 1,000 until 1970, before climbing consistently, passing 1,800 in 1980 and reaching 3,100 in 2010. National unemployment, on the other hand, rose slightly from 4% in 1950 to 4.1% in 1960, before falling steadily to 3.1% in 1980 and 1.6% in 2010."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-017-1"]},"_displayNo":"#057","summary":"Chart A below shows the rate of oil consumption (in millions of barrels) in…"},{"id":"writing-academic-022","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Trade & Industry","title":"Rotterdam port exports","prompt":"The charts give information about the amount of exports (millions of tonnes) moving through Rotterdam port in Holland to various global destinations in 2002 and 2012; and also the % rates of tax imposed on these exports by the receiving countries. Write a report summarising the information. Select and describe the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The charts show the quantity of exports being transported through Rotterdam over a ten-year period, and the level of taxation imposed on the goods at their destination.\n\nOverall, total export volumes rose slightly over the decade, but the amount going to most individual zones declined. The figures also show that the two destinations with growing export volumes also had lower tax rates in 2012.\n\nTotal exports reached 18,600 million tonnes by 2012. Europe – the largest destination – declined from 6,900 million to 6,400 million, and the USA – the second highest – fell by 300 million tonnes, or about 7%, to 3,900 million. China dipped slightly to 2,300 million, and Latin America dropped from 1,800 to 1,500 million, while the ‘other’ zones remained static at 350 million. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">By marked contrast</strong>, volumes to Asia Pacific and Australasia <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more than trebled</strong>, hitting 2,550 and 2,300 million tonnes <strong class=\"key-phrase\">respectively</strong>.\n\nTurning to taxation, we see that tax increased considerably in all the zones that showed a decline in volumes, reaching 2% in the USA, China and Latin America, and rising from 5% to 8% in Europe. The ‘other’ zones <strong class=\"key-phrase\">kept tax stable at 2%</strong>, while the growth zones reduced tax massively, from 5% to 2% in Asia Pacific and from 4% to 1% in Australasia.","phrases":[{"en":"By marked contrast","context":"Total exports reached 18,600 million tonnes by 2012. Europe – the largest destination – declined from 6,900 million to 6,400 million, and the USA – the second highest – fell by 300 million tonnes, or about 7%, to 3,900 million. China dipped slightly to 2,300 million, and Latin America dropped from 1,800 to 1,500 million, while the ‘other’ zones remained static at 350 million. By marked contrast, volumes to Asia Pacific and Australasia more than trebled, hitting 2,550 and 2,300 million tonnes respectively."},{"en":"more than trebled","context":"Total exports reached 18,600 million tonnes by 2012. Europe – the largest destination – declined from 6,900 million to 6,400 million, and the USA – the second highest – fell by 300 million tonnes, or about 7%, to 3,900 million. China dipped slightly to 2,300 million, and Latin America dropped from 1,800 to 1,500 million, while the ‘other’ zones remained static at 350 million. By marked contrast, volumes to Asia Pacific and Australasia more than trebled, hitting 2,550 and 2,300 million tonnes respectively."},{"en":"respectively","context":"Total exports reached 18,600 million tonnes by 2012. Europe – the largest destination – declined from 6,900 million to 6,400 million, and the USA – the second highest – fell by 300 million tonnes, or about 7%, to 3,900 million. China dipped slightly to 2,300 million, and Latin America dropped from 1,800 to 1,500 million, while the ‘other’ zones remained static at 350 million. By marked contrast, volumes to Asia Pacific and Australasia more than trebled, hitting 2,550 and 2,300 million tonnes respectively."},{"en":"kept tax stable at 2%","context":"Turning to taxation, we see that tax increased considerably in all the zones that showed a decline in volumes, reaching 2% in the USA, China and Latin America, and rising from 5% to 8% in Europe. The ‘other’ zones kept tax stable at 2%, while the growth zones reduced tax massively, from 5% to 2% in Asia Pacific and from 4% to 1% in Australasia."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-022-1"]},"_displayNo":"#058","summary":"Charts give information about the amount of exports (millions of tonnes)…"},{"id":"writing-academic-007","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Transport","title":"Transport incidents and injuries","prompt":"The chart below shows numbers of incidents and injuries per 100 million passenger miles travelled (PMT) by transportation type in 2002.","answer":"The bar chart compares the number of incidents and injuries for every 100 million passenger miles travelled on five different types of public transport in 2002.\n\nIt is clear that the most incidents and injuries took place on demand-response vehicles. By contrast, commuter rail services <strong class=\"key-phrase\">recorded by far the lowest figures</strong>.\n\nA total of 225 incidents and 173 injuries, per 100 million passenger miles travelled, took place on demand-response transport services. These figures were <strong class=\"key-phrase\">nearly three times as high as</strong> those for the second highest category, bus services. There were 76 incidents and 66 people were injured on buses.\n\nRail services experienced fewer problems. The number of incidents on light rail trains <strong class=\"key-phrase\">equalled the figure recorded for buses</strong>, but there were <strong class=\"key-phrase\">significantly fewer injuries</strong>, at only 39. Heavy rail services saw lower numbers of such events than light rail services, but commuter rail passengers were <strong class=\"key-phrase\">even less likely to experience problems</strong>. In fact, only 20 incidents and 17 injuries occurred on commuter trains.","phrases":[{"en":"recorded by far the lowest figures","context":"It is clear that the most incidents and injuries took place on demand-response vehicles. By contrast, commuter rail services recorded by far the lowest figures."},{"en":"nearly three times as high as","context":"A total of 225 incidents and 173 injuries, per 100 million passenger miles travelled, took place on demand-response transport services. These figures were nearly three times as high as those for the second highest category, bus services. There were 76 incidents and 66 people were injured on buses."},{"en":"equalled the figure recorded for buses","context":"Rail services experienced fewer problems. The number of incidents on light rail trains equalled the figure recorded for buses, but there were significantly fewer injuries, at only 39. Heavy rail services saw lower numbers of such events than light rail services, but commuter rail passengers were even less likely to experience problems. In fact, only 20 incidents and 17 injuries occurred on commuter trains."},{"en":"significantly fewer injuries","context":"Rail services experienced fewer problems. The number of incidents on light rail trains equalled the figure recorded for buses, but there were significantly fewer injuries, at only 39. Heavy rail services saw lower numbers of such events than light rail services, but commuter rail passengers were even less likely to experience problems. In fact, only 20 incidents and 17 injuries occurred on commuter trains."},{"en":"even less likely to experience problems","context":"Rail services experienced fewer problems. The number of incidents on light rail trains equalled the figure recorded for buses, but there were significantly fewer injuries, at only 39. Heavy rail services saw lower numbers of such events than light rail services, but commuter rail passengers were even less likely to experience problems. In fact, only 20 incidents and 17 injuries occurred on commuter trains."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-007-1"]},"_displayNo":"#059","summary":"Chart below shows numbers of incidents and injuries per 100 million…"},{"id":"writing-academic-018","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Transport","title":"Train passengers and ticket prices","prompt":"The chart below shows the number of passengers arriving at a train station per hour across a day, and also the average price (in US Dollars) of their tickets. Write a report summarising the information. Select and describe the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The chart gives data about passenger volumes reaching a station every two hours during the day, and also the average ticket cost at those times.\n\nPassenger numbers peak three times during the day, and ticket prices <strong class=\"key-phrase\">broadly follow the same pattern</strong>, rising when passenger traffic is high, except during the evening rush hour when they actually fall. Notably, the midday period combines the highest passenger total with the highest average fare.\n\nAt 6am, the number of passengers is 480, rising to 690 by 8am before falling again. There is then <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a peak of</strong> 700 at midday, followed by an afternoon decline to a low of 230 at 4pm. A third spike occurs at 6pm, reaching 670 <strong class=\"key-phrase\">before tailing off</strong>.\n\nTurning to ticket prices, the 8am passenger surge is matched by a high price of $29, followed by a peak of $31 at midday and a decrease to $17 by 4pm. However, the 6pm spike in passenger numbers <strong class=\"key-phrase\">is accompanied by</strong> a low of $11 in prices, with a subsequent rise to $22 at 8pm before falling to $18 at 10pm.","phrases":[{"en":"broadly follow the same pattern","context":"Passenger numbers peak three times during the day, and ticket prices broadly follow the same pattern, rising when passenger traffic is high, except during the evening rush hour when they actually fall. Notably, the midday period combines the highest passenger total with the highest average fare."},{"en":"a peak of","context":"At 6am, the number of passengers is 480, rising to 690 by 8am before falling again. There is then a peak of 700 at midday, followed by an afternoon decline to a low of 230 at 4pm. A third spike occurs at 6pm, reaching 670 before tailing off."},{"en":"before tailing off","context":"At 6am, the number of passengers is 480, rising to 690 by 8am before falling again. There is then a peak of 700 at midday, followed by an afternoon decline to a low of 230 at 4pm. A third spike occurs at 6pm, reaching 670 before tailing off."},{"en":"is accompanied by","context":"Turning to ticket prices, the 8am passenger surge is matched by a high price of $29, followed by a peak of $31 at midday and a decrease to $17 by 4pm. However, the 6pm spike in passenger numbers is accompanied by a low of $11 in prices, with a subsequent rise to $22 at 8pm before falling to $18 at 10pm."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-018-1"]},"_displayNo":"#060","summary":"Chart below shows the number of passengers arriving at a train station per…"},{"id":"writing-academic-023","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Transport","title":"Urban traffic speeds and urban zones","prompt":"The charts show (Chart a) the average speed of urban zone traffic (in kilometres per hour or kph) in a number of countries over a fifty year period, including a future estimate; and also (Chart b) the total number of urban zones per country (in thousands). Write a report summarising the information. Select and describe the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The charts show the average speed of city traffic in a number of countries over a fifty-year period, including projected figures for 2020, and also the number of urban zones by country.\n\nOverall, the general trend is for the speed to increase and then decrease, although speeds in three of the countries rise over the period as a whole. The data <strong class=\"key-phrase\">does not suggest any correlation</strong> between increasing urban zones and average traffic speeds.\n\nIn France, for example, the average speed increased from 60 kph in 1970 to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a peak of</strong> 86 kph in 1990, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tailing off to</strong> a forecast 52 kph in 2020. The USA also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">peaked in 1990</strong>, at 57 kph, before declining to 43 kph at the end of the period. The UK and Russia have a similar trend, rising to 56 and 72 kph <strong class=\"key-phrase\">respectively</strong> in 2010 before falling to below the starting level, and Canada shows a decline from 69 kph to 64 kph despite a peak of 73 kph. Chile, however, fluctuates around 51 kph and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remains static over the period</strong>, while Italy, Brazil and Spain, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">by contrast</strong>, show a clear rise, finishing at 58, 57 and 61 kph respectively.\n\nRegarding urban zones, the figures for all countries except Russia are projected to more than triple between 1970 and 2020. The exception is Russia, which sees a fall of about half, to 3,200.","phrases":[{"en":"does not suggest any correlation","context":"Overall, the general trend is for the speed to increase and then decrease, although speeds in three of the countries rise over the period as a whole. The data does not suggest any correlation between increasing urban zones and average traffic speeds."},{"en":"a peak of","context":"In France, for example, the average speed increased from 60 kph in 1970 to a peak of 86 kph in 1990, tailing off to a forecast 52 kph in 2020. The USA also peaked in 1990, at 57 kph, before declining to 43 kph at the end of the period. The UK and Russia have a similar trend, rising to 56 and 72 kph respectively in 2010 before falling to below the starting level, and Canada shows a decline from 69 kph to 64 kph despite a peak of 73 kph. Chile, however, fluctuates around 51 kph and remains static over the period, while Italy, Brazil and Spain, by contrast, show a clear rise, finishing at 58, 57 and 61 kph respectively."},{"en":"tailing off to","context":"In France, for example, the average speed increased from 60 kph in 1970 to a peak of 86 kph in 1990, tailing off to a forecast 52 kph in 2020. The USA also peaked in 1990, at 57 kph, before declining to 43 kph at the end of the period. The UK and Russia have a similar trend, rising to 56 and 72 kph respectively in 2010 before falling to below the starting level, and Canada shows a decline from 69 kph to 64 kph despite a peak of 73 kph. Chile, however, fluctuates around 51 kph and remains static over the period, while Italy, Brazil and Spain, by contrast, show a clear rise, finishing at 58, 57 and 61 kph respectively."},{"en":"peaked in 1990","context":"In France, for example, the average speed increased from 60 kph in 1970 to a peak of 86 kph in 1990, tailing off to a forecast 52 kph in 2020. The USA also peaked in 1990, at 57 kph, before declining to 43 kph at the end of the period. The UK and Russia have a similar trend, rising to 56 and 72 kph respectively in 2010 before falling to below the starting level, and Canada shows a decline from 69 kph to 64 kph despite a peak of 73 kph. Chile, however, fluctuates around 51 kph and remains static over the period, while Italy, Brazil and Spain, by contrast, show a clear rise, finishing at 58, 57 and 61 kph respectively."},{"en":"respectively","context":"In France, for example, the average speed increased from 60 kph in 1970 to a peak of 86 kph in 1990, tailing off to a forecast 52 kph in 2020. The USA also peaked in 1990, at 57 kph, before declining to 43 kph at the end of the period. The UK and Russia have a similar trend, rising to 56 and 72 kph respectively in 2010 before falling to below the starting level, and Canada shows a decline from 69 kph to 64 kph despite a peak of 73 kph. Chile, however, fluctuates around 51 kph and remains static over the period, while Italy, Brazil and Spain, by contrast, show a clear rise, finishing at 58, 57 and 61 kph respectively."},{"en":"remains static over the period","context":"In France, for example, the average speed increased from 60 kph in 1970 to a peak of 86 kph in 1990, tailing off to a forecast 52 kph in 2020. The USA also peaked in 1990, at 57 kph, before declining to 43 kph at the end of the period. The UK and Russia have a similar trend, rising to 56 and 72 kph respectively in 2010 before falling to below the starting level, and Canada shows a decline from 69 kph to 64 kph despite a peak of 73 kph. Chile, however, fluctuates around 51 kph and remains static over the period, while Italy, Brazil and Spain, by contrast, show a clear rise, finishing at 58, 57 and 61 kph respectively."},{"en":"by contrast","context":"In France, for example, the average speed increased from 60 kph in 1970 to a peak of 86 kph in 1990, tailing off to a forecast 52 kph in 2020. The USA also peaked in 1990, at 57 kph, before declining to 43 kph at the end of the period. The UK and Russia have a similar trend, rising to 56 and 72 kph respectively in 2010 before falling to below the starting level, and Canada shows a decline from 69 kph to 64 kph despite a peak of 73 kph. Chile, however, fluctuates around 51 kph and remains static over the period, while Italy, Brazil and Spain, by contrast, show a clear rise, finishing at 58, 57 and 61 kph respectively."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-023-1"]},"_displayNo":"#061","summary":"Charts show (Chart a) the average speed of urban zone traffic…"},{"id":"writing-academic-024","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Transport","title":"Airline crews, complaints and baggage","prompt":"The first chart below shows the number of airline crew (pilots, stewards etc) needed to fly a passenger plane between Australia and a number of other countries. The second chart shows the number of passenger complaints per plane journey made on these routes, and average kilogram (kg) weight of luggage (or baggage) carried per passenger. Write a report summarising the information. Select and describe the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The charts give data about four flight destinations from Australia in terms of crew numbers, passenger complaints made and luggage weight per passenger.\n\nOverall, it is apparent that flights to Germany require the largest crews and carry the most luggage per passenger, while flights to New Zealand are at the opposite end of the scale <strong class=\"key-phrase\">on both counts</strong>. However, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the pattern for customer complaints is reversed</strong>, with German flights attracting the fewest complaints and New Zealand flights the most. The Malaysia and Indonesia flights <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lie roughly between these two extremes</strong>.\n\nThe largest crew, of 15 people, is needed for the flight to Germany, followed by Indonesia with 11 and Malaysia with 8, while New Zealand flights need the smallest crew of only 6. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">This pattern is reflected in</strong> the weight of luggage per passenger, with Germany having the heaviest baggage at 38 kg, and Indonesia the second heaviest at 32 kg. Flights to Malaysia carry 30 kg per passenger, and New Zealand has the lightest figure at 15 kg.\n\nTurning to passenger complaints, New Zealand flights receive the most, at 6 per journey, followed by Indonesia and Malaysia with 3 each. Flights to Germany <strong class=\"key-phrase\">produce the fewest</strong>, with only 1 complaint per flight.","phrases":[{"en":"on both counts","context":"Overall, it is apparent that flights to Germany require the largest crews and carry the most luggage per passenger, while flights to New Zealand are at the opposite end of the scale on both counts. However, the pattern for customer complaints is reversed, with German flights attracting the fewest complaints and New Zealand flights the most. The Malaysia and Indonesia flights lie roughly between these two extremes."},{"en":"the pattern for customer complaints is reversed","context":"Overall, it is apparent that flights to Germany require the largest crews and carry the most luggage per passenger, while flights to New Zealand are at the opposite end of the scale on both counts. However, the pattern for customer complaints is reversed, with German flights attracting the fewest complaints and New Zealand flights the most. The Malaysia and Indonesia flights lie roughly between these two extremes."},{"en":"lie roughly between these two extremes","context":"Overall, it is apparent that flights to Germany require the largest crews and carry the most luggage per passenger, while flights to New Zealand are at the opposite end of the scale on both counts. However, the pattern for customer complaints is reversed, with German flights attracting the fewest complaints and New Zealand flights the most. The Malaysia and Indonesia flights lie roughly between these two extremes."},{"en":"This pattern is reflected in","context":"The largest crew, of 15 people, is needed for the flight to Germany, followed by Indonesia with 11 and Malaysia with 8, while New Zealand flights need the smallest crew of only 6. This pattern is reflected in the weight of luggage per passenger, with Germany having the heaviest baggage at 38 kg, and Indonesia the second heaviest at 32 kg. Flights to Malaysia carry 30 kg per passenger, and New Zealand has the lightest figure at 15 kg."},{"en":"produce the fewest","context":"Turning to passenger complaints, New Zealand flights receive the most, at 6 per journey, followed by Indonesia and Malaysia with 3 each. Flights to Germany produce the fewest, with only 1 complaint per flight."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-024-1"]},"_displayNo":"#062","summary":"First chart below shows the number of airline crew (pilots, stewards etc)…"},{"id":"writing-academic-052","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Transport","title":"Underground railway systems","prompt":"The table below gives information about the underground railway systems in six cities.","answer":"The table compares the underground rail networks in six major cities in terms of their age, size and the number of people who use them each year.\n\nIt is clear that the three oldest underground systems are larger and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">serve significantly more passengers than</strong> the newer systems.\n\nThe London underground is the oldest system, having opened in 1863. It is also the largest system, with 394 kilometres of route. The second largest system, in Paris, is only about <strong class=\"key-phrase\">half the size of</strong> the London underground, with 199 kilometres of route. However, it serves more people per year. While only third in terms of size, the Tokyo system <strong class=\"key-phrase\">is easily the most used</strong>, with 1,927 million passengers per year.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">Of the three newer networks</strong>, the Washington DC underground is the most extensive, with 126 kilometres of route, compared to only 11 kilometres and 28 kilometres for the Kyoto and Los Angeles systems. The Los Angeles network is the newest, having opened in 2001, while the Kyoto network is the smallest and serves only 45 million passengers per year.","phrases":[{"en":"serve significantly more passengers than","context":"It is clear that the three oldest underground systems are larger and serve significantly more passengers than the newer systems."},{"en":"half the size of","context":"The London underground is the oldest system, having opened in 1863. It is also the largest system, with 394 kilometres of route. The second largest system, in Paris, is only about half the size of the London underground, with 199 kilometres of route. However, it serves more people per year. While only third in terms of size, the Tokyo system is easily the most used, with 1,927 million passengers per year."},{"en":"is easily the most used","context":"The London underground is the oldest system, having opened in 1863. It is also the largest system, with 394 kilometres of route. The second largest system, in Paris, is only about half the size of the London underground, with 199 kilometres of route. However, it serves more people per year. While only third in terms of size, the Tokyo system is easily the most used, with 1,927 million passengers per year."},{"en":"Of the three newer networks","context":"Of the three newer networks, the Washington DC underground is the most extensive, with 126 kilometres of route, compared to only 11 kilometres and 28 kilometres for the Kyoto and Los Angeles systems. The Los Angeles network is the newest, having opened in 2001, while the Kyoto network is the smallest and serves only 45 million passengers per year."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-052-1"]},"_displayNo":"#063","summary":"Table below gives information about the underground railway systems in six…"},{"id":"writing-academic-067","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Transport","title":"UK commuting trends","prompt":"The graph below shows the average number of UK commuters travelling each day by car, bus or train between 1970 and 2030.","answer":"The line graph compares figures for daily travel by workers in the UK using three different forms of transport over a period of 60 years.\n\nIt is clear that the car is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">by far the most popular means of transport</strong> for UK commuters throughout the period shown. Also, while the numbers of people who use the car and train increase gradually, the number of bus users <strong class=\"key-phrase\">falls steadily</strong>.\n\nIn 1970, around 5 million UK commuters travelled by car on a daily basis, while the bus and train were used by about 4 million and 2 million people respectively. In the year 2000, the number of those driving to work rose to 7 million and the number of commuting rail passengers reached 3 million. However, there was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a small drop</strong> of approximately 0.5 million in the number of bus users.\n\nBy 2030, the number of people who commute by car is expected to reach almost 9 million, and the number of train users is also predicted to rise, to nearly 5 million. By contrast, buses <strong class=\"key-phrase\">are predicted to become a less popular choice</strong>, with only 3 million daily users.","phrases":[{"en":"by far the most popular means of transport","context":"It is clear that the car is by far the most popular means of transport for UK commuters throughout the period shown. Also, while the numbers of people who use the car and train increase gradually, the number of bus users falls steadily."},{"en":"falls steadily","context":"It is clear that the car is by far the most popular means of transport for UK commuters throughout the period shown. Also, while the numbers of people who use the car and train increase gradually, the number of bus users falls steadily."},{"en":"a small drop","context":"In 1970, around 5 million UK commuters travelled by car on a daily basis, while the bus and train were used by about 4 million and 2 million people respectively. In the year 2000, the number of those driving to work rose to 7 million and the number of commuting rail passengers reached 3 million. However, there was a small drop of approximately 0.5 million in the number of bus users."},{"en":"are predicted to become a less popular choice","context":"By 2030, the number of people who commute by car is expected to reach almost 9 million, and the number of train users is also predicted to rise, to nearly 5 million. By contrast, buses are predicted to become a less popular choice, with only 3 million daily users."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-067-1"]},"_displayNo":"#064","summary":"Graph below shows the average number of UK commuters travelling each day by…"},{"id":"writing-academic-069","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Transport","title":"Cycling to work in Britain","prompt":"The table shows changes in the number of residents cycling to work in selected areas of the United Kingdom between 2001 and 2011.","answer":"The table compares the numbers of people who cycled to work in twelve areas of the UK in the years 2001 and 2011.\n\nOverall, cycling to work increased in all twelve areas shown over the 10-year period. Inner London <strong class=\"key-phrase\">had by far the highest number</strong> of cycling commuters in both years.\n\nIn 2001, well over 43 thousand residents of inner London commuted by bicycle, and this figure rose to more than 106 thousand in 2011, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an increase of 144%</strong>. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">By contrast</strong>, although outer London had the second highest number of cycling commuters in each year, the percentage change, at only 45%, was the lowest of the twelve areas shown in the table.\n\nBrighton and Hove <strong class=\"key-phrase\">recorded the second biggest increase</strong> (109%) in the number of residents cycling to work, but Bristol had the highest number of cycling commuters among the areas shown outside London, with 8,108 in 2001 and 15,768 in 2011. Figures for the other eight areas <strong class=\"key-phrase\">were below the 10 thousand mark</strong> in both years.","phrases":[{"en":"had by far the highest number","context":"Overall, cycling to work increased in all twelve areas shown over the 10-year period. Inner London had by far the highest number of cycling commuters in both years."},{"en":"an increase of 144%","context":"In 2001, well over 43 thousand residents of inner London commuted by bicycle, and this figure rose to more than 106 thousand in 2011, an increase of 144%. By contrast, although outer London had the second highest number of cycling commuters in each year, the percentage change, at only 45%, was the lowest of the twelve areas shown in the table."},{"en":"By contrast","context":"In 2001, well over 43 thousand residents of inner London commuted by bicycle, and this figure rose to more than 106 thousand in 2011, an increase of 144%. By contrast, although outer London had the second highest number of cycling commuters in each year, the percentage change, at only 45%, was the lowest of the twelve areas shown in the table."},{"en":"recorded the second biggest increase","context":"Brighton and Hove recorded the second biggest increase (109%) in the number of residents cycling to work, but Bristol had the highest number of cycling commuters among the areas shown outside London, with 8,108 in 2001 and 15,768 in 2011. Figures for the other eight areas were below the 10 thousand mark in both years."},{"en":"were below the 10 thousand mark","context":"Brighton and Hove recorded the second biggest increase (109%) in the number of residents cycling to work, but Bristol had the highest number of cycling commuters among the areas shown outside London, with 8,108 in 2001 and 15,768 in 2011. Figures for the other eight areas were below the 10 thousand mark in both years."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-069-1"]},"_displayNo":"#065","summary":"Table below shows changes in the numbers of residents cycling to work in"},{"id":"writing-academic-034","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Water & Energy","title":"Global water use","prompt":"The graph and table below give information about water use worldwide and water consumption in two different countries.","answer":"The charts compare the amount of water used for agriculture, industry and homes around the world, and water use in Brazil and the Democratic Republic of Congo.\n\nIt is clear that global water needs <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rose significantly</strong> between 1900 and 2000, and that agriculture <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accounted for the largest proportion of</strong> water used. We can also see that water consumption was considerably higher in Brazil than in the Congo.\n\nIn 1900, around 500km³ of water was used by the agriculture sector worldwide. The figures for industrial and domestic water consumption stood at around one fifth of that amount. By 2000, global water use for agriculture <strong class=\"key-phrase\">had increased to around 3000km³</strong>, industrial water use had risen to just under half that amount, and domestic consumption had reached approximately 500km³.\n\nIn the year 2000, the populations of Brazil and the Congo were 176 million and 5.2 million <strong class=\"key-phrase\">respectively</strong>. Water consumption per person in Brazil, at 359m³, was much higher than that in the Congo, at only 8m³, while the amount of irrigated land was 26,500 km² in Brazil <strong class=\"key-phrase\">compared with</strong> 100 km² in the Congo.","phrases":[{"en":"rose significantly","context":"It is clear that global water needs rose significantly between 1900 and 2000, and that agriculture accounted for the largest proportion of water used. We can also see that water consumption was considerably higher in Brazil than in the Congo."},{"en":"accounted for the largest proportion of","context":"It is clear that global water needs rose significantly between 1900 and 2000, and that agriculture accounted for the largest proportion of water used. We can also see that water consumption was considerably higher in Brazil than in the Congo."},{"en":"had increased to around 3000km³","context":"In 1900, around 500km³ of water was used by the agriculture sector worldwide. The figures for industrial and domestic water consumption stood at around one fifth of that amount. By 2000, global water use for agriculture had increased to around 3000km³, industrial water use had risen to just under half that amount, and domestic consumption had reached approximately 500km³."},{"en":"respectively","context":"In the year 2000, the populations of Brazil and the Congo were 176 million and 5.2 million respectively. Water consumption per person in Brazil, at 359m³, was much higher than that in the Congo, at only 8m³, while the amount of irrigated land was 26,500 km² in Brazil compared with 100 km² in the Congo."},{"en":"compared with","context":"In the year 2000, the populations of Brazil and the Congo were 176 million and 5.2 million respectively. Water consumption per person in Brazil, at 359m³, was much higher than that in the Congo, at only 8m³, while the amount of irrigated land was 26,500 km² in Brazil compared with 100 km² in the Congo."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-034-1"]},"_displayNo":"#066","summary":"Graph and table below give information about water use worldwide and water…"},{"id":"writing-academic-036","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Water & Energy","title":"Water use by sector","prompt":"The pie charts compare water use in San Diego County, California and worldwide.","answer":"The pie charts give information about the water used for residential, industrial and agricultural purposes in San Diego County, California, and the world as a whole.\n\nIt is noticeable that more water is consumed by homes than by industry or agriculture in the two American regions. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">By contrast</strong>, agriculture accounts for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the vast majority of</strong> water used worldwide.\n\nIn San Diego County and California State, residential water consumption accounts for 60% and 39% of total water usage. By contrast, a mere 8% of the water used globally goes to homes. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">The opposite trend can be seen</strong> when we look at water consumption for agriculture. This accounts for a massive 69% of global water use, but only 17% and 28% of water usage in San Diego and California <strong class=\"key-phrase\">respectively</strong>.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">Such dramatic differences are not seen</strong> when we compare the figures for industrial water use. The same proportion of water (23%) is used by industry in San Diego and worldwide, while the figure for California is 10 <strong class=\"key-phrase\">percentage points</strong> higher, at 33%.","phrases":[{"en":"By contrast","context":"It is noticeable that more water is consumed by homes than by industry or agriculture in the two American regions. By contrast, agriculture accounts for the vast majority of water used worldwide."},{"en":"the vast majority of","context":"It is noticeable that more water is consumed by homes than by industry or agriculture in the two American regions. By contrast, agriculture accounts for the vast majority of water used worldwide."},{"en":"The opposite trend can be seen","context":"In San Diego County and California State, residential water consumption accounts for 60% and 39% of total water usage. By contrast, a mere 8% of the water used globally goes to homes. The opposite trend can be seen when we look at water consumption for agriculture. This accounts for a massive 69% of global water use, but only 17% and 28% of water usage in San Diego and California respectively."},{"en":"respectively","context":"In San Diego County and California State, residential water consumption accounts for 60% and 39% of total water usage. By contrast, a mere 8% of the water used globally goes to homes. The opposite trend can be seen when we look at water consumption for agriculture. This accounts for a massive 69% of global water use, but only 17% and 28% of water usage in San Diego and California respectively."},{"en":"Such dramatic differences are not seen","context":"Such dramatic differences are not seen when we compare the figures for industrial water use. The same proportion of water (23%) is used by industry in San Diego and worldwide, while the figure for California is 10 percentage points higher, at 33%."},{"en":"percentage points","context":"Such dramatic differences are not seen when we compare the figures for industrial water use. The same proportion of water (23%) is used by industry in San Diego and worldwide, while the figure for California is 10 percentage points higher, at 33%."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-036-1"]},"_displayNo":"#067","summary":"Pie charts below compare water usage in San Diego, California and"},{"id":"writing-academic-063","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Water & Energy","title":"Hospital electricity use","prompt":"The charts below compare five different countries, showing the electricity used (measured in megawatts or mw) in a typical hospital during one week, and also the number of emergency medical calls made by local residents during the same week. The last chart shows the average time (in minutes) spent on assessing emergency cases by hospital medical staff before the patients are treated. Write a report summarising the information. Select and describe the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The three charts compare the amount of electricity used in a typical hospital, the number of emergency calls made by local residents, and the time spent assessing patients before treatment, in five countries during one week.\n\nOverall, it seems that electricity use may <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reflect an increase in</strong> emergency calls made, but only in Russia does assessment time increase in the same way.\n\nRegarding electricity, we see that consumption in the French and Japanese hospitals is roughly stable during the week, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fluctuating around 32 and 18 MW respectively</strong>. In Canada and Russia, electricity use <strong class=\"key-phrase\">climbs dramatically</strong> towards the end of the week, going from 26 to 38 MW and from 12 to 24 MW. In Argentina, however, the use actually declines by almost half.\n\nThere is a similar pattern in the number of emergency calls made, which fluctuate in France (around 110 calls) and Japan (around 90). In Canada these calls rise by roughly half, from around 105 on Monday to over 160 on Sunday, with the figure in Russia showing a similar rise to 110 by the weekend. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Argentina is again the exception</strong>, with a decline of about half.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">By contrast</strong>, time spent on patient assessment is generally stable during the week in all the countries except Russia, where there is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a marked rise</strong> from Monday&#x27;s 12 minutes (which is the typical daily rate of each country except Japan) to 28 minutes on Sunday.","phrases":[{"en":"reflect an increase in","context":"Overall, it seems that electricity use may reflect an increase in emergency calls made, but only in Russia does assessment time increase in the same way."},{"en":"fluctuating around 32 and 18 MW respectively","context":"Regarding electricity, we see that consumption in the French and Japanese hospitals is roughly stable during the week, fluctuating around 32 and 18 MW respectively. In Canada and Russia, electricity use climbs dramatically towards the end of the week, going from 26 to 38 MW and from 12 to 24 MW. In Argentina, however, the use actually declines by almost half."},{"en":"climbs dramatically","context":"Regarding electricity, we see that consumption in the French and Japanese hospitals is roughly stable during the week, fluctuating around 32 and 18 MW respectively. In Canada and Russia, electricity use climbs dramatically towards the end of the week, going from 26 to 38 MW and from 12 to 24 MW. In Argentina, however, the use actually declines by almost half."},{"en":"Argentina is again the exception","context":"There is a similar pattern in the number of emergency calls made, which fluctuate in France (around 110 calls) and Japan (around 90). In Canada these calls rise by roughly half, from around 105 on Monday to over 160 on Sunday, with the figure in Russia showing a similar rise to 110 by the weekend. Argentina is again the exception, with a decline of about half."},{"en":"By contrast","context":"By contrast, time spent on patient assessment is generally stable during the week in all the countries except Russia, where there is a marked rise from Monday's 12 minutes (which is the typical daily rate of each country except Japan) to 28 minutes on Sunday."},{"en":"a marked rise","context":"By contrast, time spent on patient assessment is generally stable during the week in all the countries except Russia, where there is a marked rise from Monday's 12 minutes (which is the typical daily rate of each country except Japan) to 28 minutes on Sunday."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-063-1","writing-academic-063-2"]},"_displayNo":"#068","summary":"Charts below compare five different countries, showing the electricity used…"}]);
